INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Anatomy & Physiology','Female Reproductive Anatomy','easy','flashcard','What are the three parts of the fallopian tube from lateral to medial?','["Infundibulum (with fimbriae) → Ampulla (widest; site of fertilisation) → Isthmus → Interstitial (intramural) portion passing through the uterine wall"]','A','The fallopian tube has four parts: infundibulum (opens to peritoneal cavity via fimbriae), ampulla (widest and longest — site of fertilisation, ~70% of ectopic pregnancies), isthmus (narrow), and interstitial/intramural portion (highest rupture risk if ectopic). The tube is lined by ciliated and secretory epithelial cells.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Anatomy & Physiology','Female Reproductive Anatomy','easy','flashcard','Name the three main layers of the uterine wall from inside to outside.','["Endometrium (inner mucosa — functionalis + basalis layers) → Myometrium (thick smooth muscle — responsible for contractions) → Perimetrium (outer peritoneal/serosal layer)"]','A','The endometrium has two layers: the stratum functionalis (shed at menstruation, responds to hormones) and the stratum basalis (remains, regenerates functionalis). The myometrium contains smooth muscle arranged in interlacing bundles — contraction compresses intramyometrial vessels (living ligatures). The perimetrium is peritoneum anteriorly (vesicouterine pouch) and posteriorly (rectouterine/Douglas pouch).','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Anatomy & Physiology','Female Reproductive Anatomy','medium','flashcard','Describe the blood supply to the ovary and the clinical relevance of the ovarian vein drainage.','["Ovarian artery (from abdominal aorta at L2) and anastomotic branch of uterine artery supply the ovary. Right ovarian vein drains to the inferior vena cava directly; left ovarian vein drains to the left renal vein (at right angles — higher pressure → predisposition to left-sided varicocele equivalent and pelvic congestion)."]','A','The asymmetric ovarian venous drainage explains why isolated right-sided ovarian vein thrombosis (more common postpartum) can cause ureteric obstruction. The left ovarian vein drains to the left renal vein at a right angle, creating higher venous pressure — analogous to why varicocele is more common on the left in men (left testicular vein drains to left renal vein).','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Anatomy & Physiology','Female Reproductive Anatomy','medium','flashcard','What is the clinical significance of the relationship between the uterine artery and the ureter?','["The ureter passes approximately 1–2 cm lateral to the cervix, running beneath (''water under the bridge'') the uterine artery at the level of the internal os. This makes the ureter vulnerable to ligation or damage during hysterectomy or uterine artery ligation. Ureteric injury occurs in ~0.5% of hysterectomies."]','A','Mnemonic: ''water (ureter) under the bridge (uterine artery)''. During hysterectomy, the uterine artery is ligated close to the uterus. If ligation is too lateral or caudal, the ureter may be inadvertently included. Intraoperative cystoscopy (checking ureteric jets) is used to verify ureteric integrity after hysterectomy.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Anatomy & Physiology','Female Reproductive Anatomy','hard','flashcard','Describe the lymphatic drainage of the cervix versus the uterine body and its importance in cervical cancer staging.','["Cervix → internal iliac, external iliac, and obturator lymph nodes (also parametrial nodes). Uterine body/fundus → para-aortic (lumbar) nodes (via ovarian lymphatics) and some internal iliac nodes. Round ligament carries minor drainage to superficial inguinal nodes."]','A','Cervical cancer spreads to pelvic nodes (internal iliac, obturator, external iliac) first, hence pelvic lymphadenectomy is performed for staging. Endometrial cancer (uterine body) spreads to para-aortic nodes. This explains why sentinel lymph node mapping in cervical cancer targets obturator and internal iliac nodes, while in endometrial cancer para-aortic dissection is added for high-risk histology.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Anatomy & Physiology','Female Reproductive Anatomy','easy','flashcard','What are the two zones of the ovary and what does each contain?','["Cortex (outer): contains follicles in all stages of development embedded in stroma of spindle-shaped cells; Medulla (inner/core): contains blood vessels, lymphatics, nerves, and stromal cells but no follicles."]','A','The ovarian cortex is where folliculogenesis occurs (primordial → primary → secondary → antral → Graafian follicle). The medulla carries the blood supply via the ovarian hilum. In polycystic ovarian syndrome (PCOS), multiple small antral follicles (2–9 mm) are peripherally arranged in the cortex (''string of pearls'' on ultrasound).','universal',false,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Anatomy & Physiology','Female Reproductive Anatomy','medium','flashcard','What is the cardinal (Mackenrodt''s) ligament and what does it support?','["The cardinal ligament (transverse cervical/Mackenrodt''s ligament) is a condensation of parametrial fascia extending from the lateral cervix and upper vagina to the lateral pelvic wall. It is the primary support for the uterus against prolapse. It also contains the uterine artery, uterine veins, and lymphatics."]','A','The cardinal ligament is the most important ligament preventing uterine prolapse. Weakening (childbirth trauma, oestrogen deficiency) leads to uterovaginal prolapse. It should not be confused with the uterosacral ligament (which maintains uterine retroversion/anteversion and is important in endometriosis pain) or round ligament (which maintains anteversion).','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Anatomy & Physiology','Female Reproductive Anatomy','hard','flashcard','Describe the arterial supply of the endometrium and the functional significance of spiral arteries.','["Uterine artery → arcuate arteries (outer myometrium) → radial arteries (penetrate inward) → straight (basal) arteries (supply stratum basalis — constant) + spiral arteries (supply stratum functionalis — sensitive to progesterone withdrawal). At menstruation, spiral artery vasospasm causes ischaemia and shedding of the functionalis."]','A','Spiral arteries are uniquely sensitive to progesterone withdrawal. When progesterone falls at the end of the luteal phase, spiral arteries spasm, causing ischaemic necrosis of the functionalis → menstruation. In pregnancy, extravillous trophoblast invades and remodels spiral arteries into wide-bore, low-resistance vessels. Failure of this remodelling underlies pre-eclampsia and IUGR.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Anatomy & Physiology','Female Reproductive Anatomy','easy','flashcard','What are the zones of the endocervix vs ectocervix and what is the transformation zone?','["Ectocervix: covered by non-keratinised stratified squamous epithelium. Endocervix: lined by simple columnar (glandular) epithelium. Transformation zone (T-zone): the squamo-columnar junction (SCJ) where the two epithelia meet — the most common site of cervical intraepithelial neoplasia (CIN) and cervical carcinoma."]','A','The transformation zone is the target for cervical smears/colposcopy because metaplastic squamous cells in the T-zone are most susceptible to HPV-induced carcinogenesis. The SCJ moves with age and hormonal status: in adolescence/pregnancy it is ectocervical (ectropion/ersion); after menopause it moves up the endocervical canal (making smear sampling more difficult).','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Anatomy & Physiology','Female Reproductive Anatomy','hard','flashcard','Describe the innervation of the uterus and its clinical relevance in pain perception during labour.','["Uterus is innervated by the pelvic plexus (inferior hypogastric plexus): sympathetic fibres from T10–L1 (uterine body and cervix — transmit pain from contractions via hypogastric plexus); parasympathetic from S2–S4 (pelvic splanchnic nerves); external genitalia via pudendal nerve (S2–S4). First stage labour pain (T10–L1) is treated with epidural anaesthesia; second stage pain (S2–S4) requires sacral epidural or pudendal block."]','A','The visceral pain of uterine contractions (first stage, T10–L1) is referred to the lower back and groin. The somatic pain of perineal stretching (second stage, S2–S4) is sharp and localised. Epidural anaesthesia covers both with catheter at L3–L4. Paracervical block targets the paracervical ganglion for first-stage pain. Pudendal block targets the pudendal nerve (S2–S4) for second stage and perineal procedures.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Anatomy & Physiology','Menstrual Cycle & Ovulation','easy','flashcard','Describe the four phases of the menstrual cycle and their approximate timing in a 28-day cycle.','["Menstrual phase (days 1–5): functionalis shedding due to spiral artery vasospasm after progesterone withdrawal. Proliferative (follicular) phase (days 6–14): oestrogen-driven endometrial regrowth. Ovulation (day 14): LH surge triggers oocyte release. Secretory (luteal) phase (days 15–28): progesterone converts endometrium to secretory pattern for implantation."]','A','Key hormonal correlates: Follicular phase — rising FSH recruits follicles; rising oestradiol causes proliferative endometrium and positive feedback LH surge. Luteal phase — corpus luteum produces progesterone + oestradiol; without hCG, corpus luteum involutes at ~day 26, progesterone falls, spiral arteries spasm, menstruation ensues. The luteal phase is invariably ~14 days; variability in cycle length lies in the follicular phase.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Anatomy & Physiology','Menstrual Cycle & Ovulation','easy','flashcard','What is the LH surge and what does it trigger?','["The mid-cycle LH surge is a large, rapid rise in LH (and smaller FSH rise) triggered by sustained high oestradiol levels (>200 pg/mL for >48 hours) switching oestrogen''s feedback from negative to positive. The LH surge triggers: resumption of meiosis I in the oocyte, final follicular maturation, prostaglandin production causing follicle rupture, and ovulation ~36–48 hours later."]','A','Clinical application: ovulation predictor kits detect the urinary LH surge, which precedes ovulation by 24–48 hours (optimal for timed intercourse). The LH surge also initiates luteinisation of granulosa cells (shifting production from oestradiol to progesterone) and upregulates LH receptors on granulosa cells. A single bolus of hCG (which shares LH receptor) can trigger ovulation in IVF cycles.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Anatomy & Physiology','Menstrual Cycle & Ovulation','medium','flashcard','Compare FSH and LH levels throughout the menstrual cycle.','["Early follicular phase: FSH rises to recruit follicles; LH low. Mid follicular phase: FSH gradually falls as oestradiol/inhibin B rise (negative feedback); LH remains low. Day 12–14: both surge (LH surge dominant) triggered by high oestradiol positive feedback → ovulation. Luteal phase: both fall to low levels as corpus luteum produces oestradiol and progesterone. Late luteal: both rise again as corpus luteum involutes."]','A','The FSH fall in mid-follicular phase selects the dominant follicle (others undergo atresia from FSH deprivation). The smaller FSH surge at ovulation helps with cumulus expansion and final oocyte maturation. In the luteal phase, pulsatile LH maintains corpus luteum function. If pregnancy occurs, hCG (from trophoblast) rescues the corpus luteum by binding LH receptors, maintaining progesterone.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Anatomy & Physiology','Menstrual Cycle & Ovulation','medium','flashcard','What happens to the corpus luteum if fertilisation does not occur, and if it does?','["Without fertilisation: corpus luteum involutes after 12–14 days (luteolysis) as hCG is absent. Progesterone and oestradiol fall → FSH and LH rise → new follicle cycle begins. With fertilisation: syncytiotrophoblast secretes hCG (detectable from ~day 8 post-fertilisation) which binds LH receptors on the corpus luteum, maintaining progesterone secretion until the placenta takes over (luteo-placental shift at ~10 weeks)."]','A','The luteo-placental shift (~weeks 8–10): progesterone production transitions from the corpus luteum to the placenta. If the corpus luteum is removed before 8 weeks, progesterone must be supplemented to prevent miscarriage (used in IVF cycles with luteal phase progesterone support). After 10 weeks, the placenta produces adequate progesterone independently.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Anatomy & Physiology','Menstrual Cycle & Ovulation','hard','flashcard','Explain the two-cell, two-gonadotropin model of ovarian steroidogenesis.','["LH acts on theca interna cells → stimulates androgen production (androstenedione, testosterone) from cholesterol via cAMP/PKA. These androgens diffuse to adjacent granulosa cells. FSH acts on granulosa cells → upregulates aromatase (CYP19A1) → converts androgens to oestradiol. Neither cell type alone can produce significant oestrogen: theca cells lack aromatase; granulosa cells lack 17α-hydroxylase."]','A','Clinical correlate: In PCOS, excessive LH stimulates theca cells to overproduce androgens, but inadequate FSH-driven aromatase activity means these androgens are not converted to oestrogen → hyperandrogenaemia + anovulation. Aromatase inhibitors (letrozole) in fertility treatment: reduce oestrogen → FSH rises → stimulates follicle recruitment (first-line in PCOS).','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Anatomy & Physiology','Menstrual Cycle & Ovulation','medium','flashcard','What changes in the endometrium characterise the proliferative vs secretory phases?','["Proliferative phase (driven by oestradiol): endometrium thickens; straight tubular glands; loose stroma; oestrogen receptor expression increases. Secretory phase (driven by progesterone): glands become coiled with intraluminal secretions (glycogen); stroma becomes decidualised (pseudodecidua); spiral arteries lengthen; progesterone receptors upregulated then downregulated."]','A','The secretory phase endometrium is the ''receptive'' endometrium for implantation. The window of implantation (days 20–24) is the period of maximal receptivity marked by pinopodes (apical surface protrusions), upregulated αvβ3 integrins, and LIF. Endometrial dating (Noyes criteria) was historically used in infertility evaluation but has been replaced by molecular receptivity testing (ERA).','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Anatomy & Physiology','Menstrual Cycle & Ovulation','easy','flashcard','What is the basal body temperature (BBT) chart and how does it confirm ovulation?','["BBT is the resting morning temperature before any activity. Progesterone secreted by the corpus luteum after ovulation raises BBT by 0.3–0.5°C (thermogenic effect). A sustained rise lasting 12–14 days confirms ovulation has occurred. BBT is used retrospectively to confirm ovulation in the previous cycle — it cannot predict ovulation in advance."]','A','Clinically: BBT charts confirm ovulatory cycles but cannot predict the fertile window prospectively (for prediction, use cervical mucus monitoring or LH urine kits). A monophasic BBT (no rise) suggests anovulatory cycle. Shortened post-ovulatory phase (<10 days) suggests luteal phase defect. Temperature falls back to baseline 1–2 days before menstruation.','universal',false,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Anatomy & Physiology','Menstrual Cycle & Ovulation','hard','flashcard','Explain the neuroendocrine regulation of the menstrual cycle — the role of GnRH pulse frequency.','["GnRH is secreted in pulses from the arcuate nucleus (Kiss1 neurons key upstream regulators). Pulse frequency determines FSH vs LH response: slow pulses (every 60–90 min) preferentially stimulate FSH; fast pulses (every 60–90 min) maintain LH. In the follicular phase, increasing pulse frequency favours LH. Continuous (non-pulsatile) GnRH paradoxically suppresses LH/FSH (downregulation) — basis of GnRH agonist therapy (medical castration)."]','A','GnRH agonists (leuprolide, goserelin) initially cause a ''flare'' of LH/FSH, then receptor downregulation causes oestrogen fall — used in endometriosis, prostate cancer, and IVF pituitary suppression. GnRH antagonists (cetrorelix, ganirelix) immediately block receptors without flare — used in IVF protocols. Pulsatile GnRH (pump therapy) is used to restore fertility in hypogonadotrophic hypogonadism.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Anatomy & Physiology','Menstrual Cycle & Ovulation','medium','flashcard','What is dysmenorrhoea and what is its hormonal mechanism?','["Primary dysmenorrhoea: painful menstruation without pelvic pathology. Caused by excess prostaglandins (PGE2, PGF2α) from the endometrium following progesterone withdrawal. PGF2α causes myometrial contractions and uterine ischaemia. Treatment: NSAIDs (COX inhibitors reduce prostaglandin production) and combined oral contraceptive pill (suppresses ovulation, thins endometrium → less prostaglandin)."]','A','Secondary dysmenorrhoea has an underlying cause: endometriosis (most common in adults), adenomyosis, fibroids, or pelvic inflammatory disease. Primary dysmenorrhoea typically begins within 1–2 years of menarche (with onset of ovulatory cycles) and is relieved by pregnancy. NSAIDs are most effective when started 1–2 days before the period. Prostaglandin excess also causes nausea, diarrhoea, and vasovagal symptoms in severe cases.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Anatomy & Physiology','Menstrual Cycle & Ovulation','hard','flashcard','Explain the concept of follicular reserve and how it is assessed clinically.','["Follicular reserve refers to the quantity and quality of remaining primordial follicles in the ovarian cortex. Women are born with ~1–2 million primordial follicles; by puberty ~300,000 remain; by menopause <1000 remain. Clinical assessment: Anti-Müllerian hormone (AMH) secreted by preantral/small antral follicle granulosa cells — remains constant throughout the cycle; antral follicle count (AFC) on day 2–5 ultrasound; early follicular FSH (rises as follicle pool depletes)."]','A','AMH is the best single marker of ovarian reserve: does not vary with cycle phase, is independent of FSH/LH. Low AMH + high FSH = poor ovarian reserve (poor response to IVF stimulation). AMH also produced by fetal testicular Sertoli cells — involved in Müllerian duct regression in male fetal development (absence of AMH in females allows Müllerian duct persistence → uterus and fallopian tubes).','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Anatomy & Physiology','Female Sex Hormones','easy','flashcard','List the main physiological effects of oestrogen on reproductive tissues.','["Uterus: stimulates endometrial proliferation; increases myometrial oxytocin receptors; increases myometrial gap junctions. Fallopian tubes: increases ciliary beat frequency (helps egg transport). Cervix: thin, watery, copious mucus with ferning pattern (allows sperm penetration). Vagina: thickens epithelium (glycogen → lactic acid → acidic pH → antimicrobial). Breasts: ductal growth and fat deposition."]','A','Mnemonic for oestrogen effects — PRIMES: Proliferative endometrium, Receptor for progesterone (upregulated), Increased LH surge (positive feedback at high levels), Mammary ductal growth, Electrolyte/water retention (mild), Skeletal growth and epiphyseal fusion. Oestrogen deficiency after menopause → urogenital atrophy (GSM), osteoporosis, hot flushes, cardiovascular risk increase.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Anatomy & Physiology','Female Sex Hormones','easy','flashcard','List the main physiological effects of progesterone.','["Endometrium: converts proliferative to secretory pattern; decidualisation. Myometrium: quiescence (reduces gap junctions, suppresses oxytocin receptors). Cervix: thick, scant, cellular mucus (blocks sperm). BBT: thermogenic effect (+0.3–0.5°C). Breast: alveolar development (mammogenesis). Respiratory: stimulates respiratory centre (hyperpnoea). Renal: mild natriuresis (competes with aldosterone). CNS: sedative effect; increases appetite."]','A','Progesterone''s sedative and thermogenic effects explain fatigue and raised temperature in the luteal phase and early pregnancy. Its competition with aldosterone (natriuresis) is counteracted by the marked aldosterone rise in pregnancy, maintaining sodium balance. Synthetic progestins in the combined OCP reduce endometrial cancer risk and provide most of the contraceptive effect by suppressing LH surge.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Anatomy & Physiology','Female Sex Hormones','medium','flashcard','Explain the negative and positive feedback of oestrogen on the hypothalamic-pituitary axis.','["NEGATIVE feedback (most of cycle): oestradiol at physiological levels suppresses GnRH and pituitary FSH/LH release. POSITIVE feedback (pre-ovulatory only): when oestradiol exceeds ~200 pg/mL for >48 hours, it SWITCHES to stimulate the anterior pituitary and hypothalamus, triggering the mid-cycle LH surge. After ovulation, progesterone reinforces negative feedback in the luteal phase."]','A','The switch from negative to positive feedback is unique to females. It requires a critical oestradiol level AND duration of exposure. Mechanism is not fully understood but involves upregulation of GnRH receptor sensitivity, upregulation of pituitary LH stores, and kisspeptin neuron activation in the AVPV nucleus. Anovulatory cycles (e.g., low BMI, stress) fail to generate the required oestradiol peak to trigger the LH surge.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Anatomy & Physiology','Female Sex Hormones','medium','flashcard','What are the three oestrogens and which is produced by the placenta?','["Three naturally occurring oestrogens: Oestradiol (E2) — most potent, produced by ovaries (and fetoplacental unit in pregnancy). Oestrone (E1) — less potent, predominant post-menopausally (from peripheral aromatisation of androstenedione). Oestriol (E3) — weakest, produced uniquely by placenta using 16α-hydroxy-DHEA-S from fetal adrenal/liver (the fetoplacental unit product). Low oestriol in second trimester screen suggests trisomy 18 or placental insufficiency."]','A','After menopause, oestrone becomes the dominant oestrogen (from aromatisation in adipose tissue). Obese postmenopausal women have higher oestrone levels, conferring some protection from osteoporosis but increasing endometrial and breast cancer risk. Oestriol (E3) is used in topical vaginal preparations for genitourinary syndrome of menopause (GSM) — minimal systemic absorption, high local activity.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Anatomy & Physiology','Female Sex Hormones','hard','flashcard','Describe the steroidogenic pathway from cholesterol to oestradiol.','["Cholesterol → (CYP11A1/desmolase) → Pregnenolone → (3β-HSD) → Progesterone → (CYP17A1: 17α-hydroxylase/17,20-lyase) → Androstenedione → (17β-HSD) → Testosterone → (CYP19A1: aromatase) → Oestradiol. Key enzymes: StAR protein (rate-limiting, cholesterol transport); CYP17A1 (essential for sex steroid pathway — absent in placenta); Aromatase (in granulosa cells, placenta, adipose, brain)."]','A','Enzyme deficiency consequences: CYP11A1 deficiency → no steroid production → salt-wasting, 46,XY female phenotype. 3β-HSD deficiency → impaired cortisol, sex steroids → CAH with salt loss and ambiguous genitalia. CYP17A1 deficiency → no sex steroids, no cortisol, excess mineralocorticoids → hypertension, hypogonadism. Aromatase deficiency → virilisation of female fetus, maternal virilisation, no oestrogen postnatally.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Anatomy & Physiology','Female Sex Hormones','medium','flashcard','How does SHBG regulate the bioavailability of sex hormones?','["SHBG (sex hormone-binding globulin), produced by the liver, binds oestradiol and testosterone with high affinity. Only the free (unbound) fraction is biologically active. Factors increasing SHBG: oestrogen, thyroid hormone, liver disease, weight loss, OCP use. Factors decreasing SHBG: androgens, insulin (→ more free testosterone in PCOS), obesity, hypothyroidism, GH excess. In PCOS, high insulin reduces SHBG → more free testosterone → hyperandrogenaemia."]','A','SHBG measurement is clinically important in PCOS: even if total testosterone is mildly elevated, low SHBG amplifies the free testosterone level. The Free Androgen Index (FAI) = total testosterone × 100 / SHBG. The combined OCP raises SHBG (via hepatic oestrogen effect), reducing free testosterone — explaining why it improves hirsutism and acne in PCOS patients.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Anatomy & Physiology','Female Sex Hormones','hard','flashcard','Explain the mechanism of action of oestrogen receptors (ERα and ERβ).','["Oestrogen diffuses into cells and binds nuclear oestrogen receptors (ERα, ERβ). Ligand binding causes receptor dimerisation and binding to oestrogen response elements (EREs) in DNA promoter regions → gene transcription. ERα predominates in uterus, breast, bone, liver, pituitary. ERβ predominates in ovary, colon, brain, lung. Non-genomic effects occur via membrane-associated ER activating kinase signalling cascades (rapid)."]','A','Selective oestrogen receptor modulators (SERMs) exploit differential receptor distribution and coregulator expression: Tamoxifen = antagonist breast (reduces cancer risk), agonist bone and uterus (endometrial cancer risk). Raloxifene = antagonist breast and uterus (no endometrial cancer risk), agonist bone and lipids. Aromatase inhibitors (anastrozole, letrozole) block oestrogen synthesis entirely, used in postmenopausal breast cancer treatment.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Anatomy & Physiology','Female Sex Hormones','easy','flashcard','What are the effects of oestrogen on the cardiovascular system?','["Beneficial effects: raises HDL cholesterol; lowers LDL cholesterol; promotes vasodilation (via nitric oxide and prostacyclin); inhibits vascular smooth muscle proliferation; anti-inflammatory. Adverse effects: promotes coagulation (raises factors VII, VIII, X, fibrinogen; reduces protein S) and increases VTE risk; at high doses may raise triglycerides."]','A','The cardiovascular benefits of oestrogen explain the lower cardiovascular risk in premenopausal women vs age-matched men. After menopause, cardiovascular risk increases. However, the Women''s Health Initiative (WHI) showed combined HRT (oestrogen + progestogen) increased cardiovascular events in older women — the ''timing hypothesis'' suggests benefits occur if HRT is started within 10 years of menopause (''window of opportunity'') but harm if started later (in established atherosclerosis).','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Anatomy & Physiology','Female Sex Hormones','medium','flashcard','How does the combined oral contraceptive pill (COCP) work?','["COCP contains synthetic oestrogen (ethinylestradiol) + progestogen. Mechanisms: (1) Inhibition of FSH and LH by negative feedback → anovulation (primary mechanism). (2) Thickening of cervical mucus → sperm penetration blocked. (3) Thinning of endometrium → reduced implantation potential. (4) Altered fallopian tube motility. Efficacy: >99% with perfect use."]','A','The progestogen component provides most of the contraceptive effect via LH suppression (preventing ovulation) and cervical mucus thickening. The oestrogen prevents breakthrough bleeding and supports the cycle. Missed pills: if >24–48 hours late (depending on progestogen), LH may rise and ovulation may occur — use additional contraception for 7 days. Emergency contraception (Levonorgestrel 1.5 mg) works mainly by delaying/inhibiting ovulation.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Anatomy & Physiology','Female Sex Hormones','hard','flashcard','Describe the features and pathophysiology of polycystic ovarian syndrome (PCOS).','["PCOS is characterised by: hyperandrogenaemia (hirsutism, acne, alopecia), oligo/anovulation, and polycystic ovarian morphology (≥20 follicles per ovary or ovarian volume >10 mL). Pathophysiology: insulin resistance → elevated insulin → reduced SHBG + stimulates ovarian theca cell androgen production → hyperandrogenaemia. Elevated LH:FSH ratio → excess LH drives theca androgen production; insufficient FSH-driven aromatase → anovulation."]','A','Diagnosis (Rotterdam 2003): ≥2 of: oligo/anovulation, clinical/biochemical hyperandrogenaemia, polycystic ovarian morphology on ultrasound. Management: OCP (reduces LH, increases SHBG, manages acne/hirsutism); metformin (improves insulin resistance, restores ovulation); letrozole (first-line ovulation induction in PCOS per NICE/ESHRE). Long-term risks: type 2 diabetes, endometrial cancer (from unopposed oestrogen in anovulatory cycles).','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Anatomy & Physiology','Fertilisation & Early Embryology','easy','flashcard','Describe the steps of fertilisation from sperm-egg contact to zygote formation.','["(1) Sperm penetrates corona radiata (hyaluronidase). (2) Capacitated sperm binds ZP3 on zona pellucida → acrosome reaction (releases acrosin, hyaluronidase). (3) Sperm penetrates zona pellucida. (4) Sperm plasma membrane fuses with oocyte plasma membrane → cortical granule exocytosis (zona reaction — permanent block to polyspermy). (5) Oocyte completes meiosis II (was arrested at metaphase II) → second polar body extruded. (6) Male and female pronuclei fuse → zygote (2n)."]','A','The cortical reaction is triggered by IP3-mediated calcium waves in the oocyte cytoplasm after sperm entry. Calcium oscillations activate the oocyte and drive the cortical granule exocytosis. In vitro fertilisation (IVF): conventional IVF fails in severe oligospermia because zona penetration is impaired → intracytoplasmic sperm injection (ICSI) directly injects a single sperm into the oocyte cytoplasm, bypassing all natural sperm-oocyte barriers.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Anatomy & Physiology','Fertilisation & Early Embryology','easy','flashcard','Describe the timeline of early embryonic development from fertilisation to implantation.','["Day 0: Fertilisation (ampulla of fallopian tube). Day 1: Zygote → 2-cell stage. Days 2–3: Morula (12–16 cells, solid ball) enters uterine cavity. Days 4–5: Blastocyst forms (ICM + trophoblast + blastocoele cavity); zona pellucida hatches. Days 6–10: Blastocyst implants (most commonly posterior uterine fundus). Syncytiotrophoblast invades decidua; hCG secreted from day 6–8."]','A','Implantation timing is critical: the window of implantation (days 20–24 of a 28-day cycle) requires synchrony between blastocyst maturation and endometrial receptivity. A blastocyst arriving too early (thick zona) or too late (unreceptive endometrium) will fail to implant. IVF embryo transfer timing: day-5 blastocysts have higher implantation rates than day-3 cleavage embryos because they are better selected and self-synchronised with the endometrium.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Anatomy & Physiology','Fertilisation & Early Embryology','medium','flashcard','What is sperm capacitation and where does it occur?','["Capacitation is the physiological process that renders sperm capable of fertilising an egg. It occurs in the female reproductive tract (cervical mucus, uterine/tubal fluid) over 5–6 hours. Changes: (1) Removal of cholesterol from sperm plasma membrane (destabilises membrane). (2) Increased intracellular cAMP and calcium. (3) Protein tyrosine phosphorylation (activates signalling). (4) Hyperactivated motility (whiplash flagellar movement). (5) Primes sperm for acrosome reaction."]','A','Clinically: in vitro capacitation is performed in IVF lab by ''washing'' sperm in culture media and incubation. Swim-up or density gradient centrifugation selects capacitated motile sperm. Sperm from semen cannot directly fertilise eggs — they must be capacitated first. In the female tract, capacitation takes hours; the timing window (sperm can survive 3–5 days in receptive cervical mucus) allows for fertilisation even if intercourse precedes ovulation by several days.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Anatomy & Physiology','Fertilisation & Early Embryology','medium','flashcard','What is the trophoblast and what are its two main subtypes?','["Trophoblast: outer cell layer of the blastocyst that gives rise to all placental and extra-embryonic structures. Two subtypes: (1) Cytotrophoblast (CTB): mononuclear stem cells; continue to proliferate; give rise to syncytiotrophoblast and extravillous trophoblast. (2) Syncytiotrophoblast (STB): multinucleated; formed by CTB fusion; non-proliferating; directly contacts maternal blood; produces hCG, hPL, progesterone, oestrogens. Extravillous CTB invades decidua and spiral arteries."]','A','Gestational trophoblastic disease (GTD): abnormal trophoblast proliferation. Hydatidiform mole: abnormal fertilisation → trophoblast proliferates without viable fetus; very high hCG; treated with suction curettage. Complete mole (46,XX — all paternal): higher risk of malignant transformation. Partial mole (69,XXY — triploid): lower risk. Choriocarcinoma: malignant GTD; highly chemosensitive (methotrexate, EMA-CO).','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Anatomy & Physiology','Fertilisation & Early Embryology','hard','flashcard','Describe the three phases of implantation.','["(1) Apposition: loose initial contact between trophoblast and endometrial epithelium (days 6–7). (2) Adhesion: firm attachment mediated by integrins (αvβ3) on trophoblast binding osteopontin/fibronectin on endometrium; L-selectin interactions; requires pinopodes. (3) Invasion: syncytiotrophoblast and extravillous CTB penetrate endometrial epithelium → decidua → myometrium → spiral arteries (endovascular EVT). Decidua produces LIF, interleukins, and growth factors facilitating invasion."]','A','Immune tolerance at implantation: the semi-allogeneic embryo must evade maternal immune rejection. Mechanisms: HLA-G expression on extravillous trophoblast inhibits NK cell killing; regulatory T cells (Tregs) expanded by progesterone and LIF; prostaglandin E2 has local immunosuppressive effects. Failure of immune tolerance is implicated in recurrent miscarriage. Low-dose aspirin and progesterone supplementation are used therapeutically in recurrent pregnancy loss.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Anatomy & Physiology','Fertilisation & Early Embryology','medium','flashcard','What is the significance of X-inactivation (lyonisation) in female embryos?','["In female embryos (46,XX), one X chromosome in each somatic cell is randomly inactivated early in development (at the ~16-cell stage) forming a Barr body (condensed inactive X/Xi). Inactivation is random but clonal: all descendants of a cell share the same inactive X. This equalises gene dosage between XX females and XY males. Females are therefore mosaics for X-linked gene expression."]','A','Clinical significance: Female carriers of X-linked recessive conditions (haemophilia, Duchenne muscular dystrophy) are usually unaffected because cells with the normal X active predominate. However, due to random lyonisation, carrier females can sometimes be symptomatic (manifesting carriers). Turner syndrome (45,X) has no second X to inactivate — the single active X is insufficient for normal gonadal development, explaining streak gonads.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Anatomy & Physiology','Fertilisation & Early Embryology','easy','flashcard','What is the decidua and what are its three zones?','["The decidua is the specialised endometrium of pregnancy (undergoes decidualisation under progesterone influence). Three zones: (1) Decidua basalis: beneath the implanting embryo; becomes the maternal component of the placenta. (2) Decidua capsularis: covers the implanting embryo (on the uterine cavity side). (3) Decidua parietalis (vera): lines the rest of the uterine cavity. Decidua capsularis and parietalis fuse by ~12 weeks as the amniotic cavity expands."]','A','Decidual natural killer (dNK) cells are abundant in the decidua basalis and play a key role in facilitating trophoblast invasion and spiral artery remodelling (dNK cells produce cytokines and matrix metalloproteinases that support EVT invasion). This is distinct from blood NK cells which would kill foreign cells. In recurrent miscarriage, abnormal dNK cell populations and impaired trophoblast invasion are found.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Anatomy & Physiology','Fertilisation & Early Embryology','hard','flashcard','What are the three germ layers formed during gastrulation and what do they each give rise to?','["Ectoderm (outer): CNS, peripheral nervous system, skin epidermis, hair, nails, teeth enamel, lens, ear epithelia, adrenal medulla. Mesoderm (middle): muscle (skeletal, cardiac, smooth), connective tissue, bone, cartilage, kidney, urogenital tract, cardiovascular system, serous linings (pleura, pericardium, peritoneum), spleen, adrenal cortex. Endoderm (inner): gut epithelium, liver, pancreas, respiratory epithelium, urinary bladder epithelium, thyroid, parathyroids, thymus."]','A','Gastrulation occurs at week 3 with formation of the primitive streak on the epiblast surface of the bilaminar germ disc. Epiblast cells migrate through the primitive streak to form mesoderm (between epiblast/ectoderm and hypoblast/endoderm) and displace hypoblast cells to form definitive endoderm. Teratogens acting during gastrulation (weeks 2–8, the critical period) cause the most severe malformations.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Anatomy & Physiology','Fertilisation & Early Embryology','medium','flashcard','What is an ectopic pregnancy? Where does it most commonly occur and what is the management?','["Ectopic pregnancy: implantation outside the uterine cavity (~1–2% of pregnancies). Most common site: fallopian tube ampulla (~70%). Other sites: isthmus (10%), cornual/interstitial (2–4% — highest mortality), ovary (3%), abdominal (1%), cervical (1%). Management: if haemodynamically stable and small — methotrexate (folic acid antagonist kills rapidly dividing trophoblast); surgical — laparoscopic salpingectomy (preferred) or salpingostomy if only tube remains."]','A','Risk factors for ectopic pregnancy: previous ectopic, salpingitis/PID (tubal scarring from Chlamydia), previous tubal surgery, IUD use, assisted reproduction, smoking. Symptoms: triad of lower abdominal pain, vaginal bleeding, and amenorrhoea. Rising hCG but lower than expected for gestational age. On ultrasound: empty uterus with adnexal mass. Cornual ectopics are most dangerous — the abundant myometrium delays rupture until a larger size is reached, causing massive haemorrhage.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Anatomy & Physiology','Fertilisation & Early Embryology','hard','flashcard','Describe fetal sex determination from genotype to phenotype.','["(1) Genetic sex: XX (female) vs XY (male) determined at fertilisation. (2) Gonadal sex: SRY gene on Y chromosome activates testis-determining factor (TDF) → testes form (week 7). Without SRY → ovaries form by default. (3) Phenotypic sex: Testes → testosterone (Leydig cells) → Wolffian duct (epididymis, vas, seminal vesicles) development. Testes → AMH (Sertoli cells) → Müllerian duct regression. DHT (from testosterone via 5α-reductase) → external genitalia masculinisation. Without testes → Müllerian structures persist (uterus, tubes) → female external genitalia by default."]','A','Disorders of sex development (DSD): (1) Complete androgen insensitivity (CAIS): 46,XY, normal testes, AR mutation → female phenotype, absent uterus (AMH present), blind vagina. (2) Congenital adrenal hyperplasia: 46,XX, excess adrenal androgens → virilisation (most common cause of ambiguous genitalia in XX). (3) 5α-reductase deficiency: 46,XY, female phenotype at birth, virilises at puberty (testosterone rise overcomes DHT deficiency). (4) Kallmann syndrome: failure of GnRH neuron migration from olfactory placode.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Anatomy & Physiology','Placental Physiology','easy','flashcard','Describe the structure of the placenta at term.','["At term: discoid organ ~500 g, 15–20 cm diameter, 2.5 cm thick. Fetal surface (smooth, shiny): covered by amnion; contains chorionic plate from which villi arise and umbilical vessels branch. Maternal surface (rough, 15–20 lobes/cotyledons): dark red, lobulated decidua basalis. Circulation: fetal blood in chorionic villi capillaries; maternal blood bathes villi in intervillous space (uteroplacental circulation). Umbilical cord: 50–60 cm long; 2 arteries + 1 vein (AVA mnemonic: Arteries carry Away, Vein carries back)."]','A','Single umbilical artery (SUA): associated with renal agenesis, IUGR, and chromosomal anomalies (trisomy 18) — screen with detailed fetal anomaly ultrasound. Vasa praevia: fetal vessels crossing the internal os unprotected by placenta/cord — risk of fetal haemorrhage with membrane rupture. Velamentous cord insertion: umbilical cord inserts into membranes rather than placenta — vessels are unprotected and susceptible to rupture.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Anatomy & Physiology','Placental Physiology','easy','flashcard','What are the hormones produced by the placenta and their functions?','["hCG: maintains corpus luteum progesterone production until luteo-placental shift (~10 weeks); basis of pregnancy tests. hPL: GH analogue; insulin resistance/diabetogenic; lipolysis; mammary gland preparation. Progesterone: uterine quiescence; immunosuppression; breast development. Oestrogens (mainly oestriol): uterine growth; breast development; upregulates oxytocin receptors at term. CRH: rises exponentially near term; prepares uterus for labour."]','A','Placenta is also an endocrine organ producing: relaxin (joint laxity, cervical ripening, renal vasodilation), PTH-related protein (PTHrP — regulates fetal calcium homeostasis and placental calcium transfer), IGF-2 (major fetal growth factor, imprinted — expressed from paternal allele only). Placental alkaline phosphatase (PALP) is a marker of placental function and elevated in cholestasis of pregnancy.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Anatomy & Physiology','Placental Physiology','medium','flashcard','How does the placenta transfer oxygen and carbon dioxide?','["O2 transfer: Simple diffusion; maternal PO2 in intervillous space (~50 mmHg) > fetal umbilical vein PO2 (~30 mmHg). HbF has higher O2 affinity (leftward shifted ODC) than HbA → facilitates O2 loading from mother to fetus. Double Bohr effect augments transfer. CO2 transfer: Simple diffusion; fetal PCO2 (~48 mmHg) > maternal PCO2 (~40 mmHg). CO2 moves fetal→maternal. Also transferred as HCO3- and carbamino compounds."]','A','The Haldane effect also operates: as O2 loads onto HbF, deoxy-HbA releases CO2 more readily (maternal blood better carries CO2 away). The double Bohr + double Haldane effects together make placental gas exchange highly efficient despite lower partial pressure gradients than pulmonary alveoli. Fetal oxygen saturation at term is ~65–75% in the umbilical vein — lower than adult arterial saturation but adequate because fetal cardiac output is higher relative to body size.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Anatomy & Physiology','Placental Physiology','medium','flashcard','What is the fetoplacental unit and why is it important for oestrogen synthesis in pregnancy?','["The fetoplacental unit: placenta + fetal adrenal/liver work together to synthesise oestriol (E3) — neither alone can do so. Pathway: Fetal adrenal cortex (fetal zone) → secretes DHEA-S. Fetal liver → 16α-hydroxylates DHEA-S to 16α-OH-DHEA-S. Placenta → aromatises 16α-OH-DHEA-S to oestriol (E3). Reason: placenta lacks CYP17A1 (cannot make DHEA-S); fetal adrenal lacks aromatase."]','A','Clinical significance: oestriol is a marker of fetoplacental function. In the second trimester triple/quad screen: low unconjugated oestriol (uE3) suggests: trisomy 18 (Edward syndrome), trisomy 21, Smith-Lemli-Opitz syndrome (cholesterol synthesis defect), placental sulphatase deficiency, or fetal adrenal insufficiency. Steroid sulphatase deficiency in the fetus impairs conversion of DHEA-S to DHEA → very low oestriol; affected males also develop X-linked ichthyosis postnatally.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Anatomy & Physiology','Placental Physiology','hard','flashcard','Describe the pathophysiology of pre-eclampsia from placental insufficiency to systemic disease.','["(1) Deficient trophoblast invasion → incomplete spiral artery remodelling → narrow high-resistance vessels → uteroplacental ischaemia. (2) Ischaemic placenta releases anti-angiogenic factors: soluble Flt-1 (sFlt-1, traps VEGF and PlGF) and soluble endoglin (sEng, traps TGF-β). (3) Excess sFlt-1 blocks endothelial VEGF signalling → widespread endothelial dysfunction. (4) Endothelial dysfunction → vasoconstriction (hypertension) + glomerular endotheliosis (proteinuria) + platelet activation + coagulation activation → HELLP syndrome."]','A','Pre-eclampsia screening: first trimester combined screen (uterine artery Doppler PI + PlGF + PAPP-A + MAP + BMI) identifies high-risk women. Low-dose aspirin (75–150 mg/day) from 12–16 weeks reduces pre-eclampsia risk in high-risk women by ~60% (COX inhibition → reduced thromboxane A2 → improved platelet/trophoblast function and vasodilation). Definition: new-onset hypertension ≥140/90 after 20 weeks with proteinuria ≥300 mg/24h or other organ involvement.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Anatomy & Physiology','Placental Physiology','medium','flashcard','How does the placenta transfer immunoglobulins to the fetus?','["IgG is the only immunoglobulin transferred across the placenta (by receptor-mediated transcytosis via the neonatal Fc receptor, FcRn, on syncytiotrophoblast). Transfer begins at ~16 weeks and accelerates in the third trimester. At term, fetal IgG levels equal or exceed maternal levels. IgA (in breast milk/colostrum), IgM, IgE, and IgD do NOT cross the placenta."]','A','Clinical significance: Maternal IgG antibodies provide passive neonatal immunity for 3–6 months (protection against measles, rubella, tetanus — explains maternal vaccination benefit for the newborn). Harmful maternal IgG also crosses: anti-D (haemolytic disease of newborn), TSH receptor antibodies (neonatal thyrotoxicosis), anti-Ro/La (neonatal lupus and congenital heart block), anti-platelet antibodies (neonatal alloimmune thrombocytopenia). Rhesus prophylaxis: anti-D immunoglobulin given to Rh-negative mothers prevents sensitisation.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Anatomy & Physiology','Placental Physiology','easy','flashcard','What is the luteo-placental shift and when does it occur?','["The luteo-placental shift is the transition of progesterone production from the corpus luteum to the syncytiotrophoblast of the placenta, occurring at approximately 8–10 weeks of gestation. Before the shift, the corpus luteum (maintained by hCG) is the primary progesterone source. After ~10 weeks, hCG declines and placental progesterone production is sufficient to maintain pregnancy."]','A','Clinical relevance: Surgical removal of the corpus luteum before 8 weeks will cause miscarriage unless exogenous progesterone is given. After 10 weeks, oophorectomy does not threaten pregnancy. In IVF with frozen embryo transfer cycles (no corpus luteum), progesterone supplementation (pessaries, injections) must continue until ~12 weeks when the luteo-placental shift is complete. hCG production by the placenta maintains the corpus luteum by binding LH receptors.','universal',false,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Anatomy & Physiology','Placental Physiology','hard','flashcard','Describe placenta praevia — types, risk factors, and management.','["Placenta praevia: placenta implanted over or close to the internal cervical os. Types: Complete (major) praevia — placenta completely covers os (deliver by CS); Partial — partially covers os; Marginal — edge reaches os; Low-lying — within 2 cm of os. Risk factors: previous CS (uterine scar → abnormal placentation), multiparity, IVF, advanced maternal age, previous uterine surgery, smoking. Presentation: painless bright red antepartum haemorrhage (after 20 weeks). Management: caesarean section if complete; monitor if low-lying."]','A','Placenta accreta spectrum (PAS): abnormal trophoblast invasion through decidua basalis into myometrium (accreta), through myometrium (increta), or through serosa/adjacent organs (percreta). Major risk factor: previous CS with placenta praevia (risk ~25% with ≥2 prior CS + praevia). Can cause catastrophic haemorrhage at delivery. Management: planned CS hysterectomy at 34–36 weeks with multidisciplinary team, cell salvage, and interventional radiology on standby.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Anatomy & Physiology','Placental Physiology','medium','flashcard','How does the placenta regulate fetal calcium homeostasis?','["Calcium is actively transported from mother to fetus against a concentration gradient (fetal ionised calcium > maternal). Mechanism: calcium enters syncytiotrophoblast via TRPV6 calcium channels on maternal-facing membrane, is bound by calbindin-D9k intracellularly, and exported via PMCA3 calcium ATPase on fetal-facing membrane. PTHrP (parathyroid hormone-related protein) produced by placenta and fetal parathyroid glands drives active calcium transport."]','A','Fetal calcium demand is highest in the third trimester (mineralisation of fetal skeleton). Maternal adaptations: increased intestinal calcium absorption (via increased 1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D / calcitriol production), decreased renal calcium excretion, and mobilisation from maternal bone. Maternal vitamin D deficiency impairs intestinal calcium absorption → neonatal rickets (even if maternal serum calcium is maintained at expense of maternal bone density). Routine vitamin D supplementation in pregnancy is recommended.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Anatomy & Physiology','Placental Physiology','hard','flashcard','What is fetal growth restriction (FGR) and how does placental pathology cause it?','["FGR: fetal weight below 10th centile (severe <3rd centile) for gestational age. Placental causes (uteroplacental insufficiency — most common in early-onset FGR): defective spiral artery remodelling → reduced uteroplacental blood flow → placental insufficiency → reduced nutrient/oxygen delivery → fetal brain-sparing (redistributed circulation to brain, heart, adrenals at expense of gut, kidneys, limbs) → oligohydramnios + asymmetric FGR. Velamentous cord insertion, massive perivillous fibrin deposition, chronic villitis also cause FGR."]','A','Fetal surveillance in FGR: umbilical artery Doppler (absent/reversed end-diastolic flow = most severe — imminent fetal compromise), middle cerebral artery Doppler (brain-sparing: low pulsatility index), ductus venosus Doppler (absent/reversed a-wave = cardiac decompensation), CTG/BPP, growth scans. Timing of delivery is individualised based on severity and gestation. Antenatal corticosteroids given for lung maturation if preterm delivery anticipated.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Anatomy & Physiology','Pregnancy Physiology','easy','flashcard','Summarise the cardiovascular changes in normal pregnancy.','["Heart rate: +15–20 bpm. Stroke volume: +25–30%. Cardiac output: +40–50% (peaks 28–32 weeks). Systemic vascular resistance: decreases ~30% (oestrogen, progesterone, nitric oxide, prostaglandins). Blood pressure: falls 5–10 mmHg in first/second trimester (vasodilation predominates); returns to normal in third trimester. Plasma volume: +40–50%. Red cell mass: +20–30% → physiological anaemia (haemodilution). Fibrinogen and clotting factors: increase → hypercoagulable state."]','A','Auscultatory findings in normal pregnancy: flow (ejection) murmur at left sternal edge is common (high cardiac output, anaemia, increased blood flow). S1 may be louder; S3 may be audible. These are physiological. Diastolic murmurs and loud systolic murmurs warrant investigation. Aortocaval compression in the supine position causes supine hypotension syndrome → position pregnant women in left lateral tilt (15–30°) from 20 weeks onward.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Anatomy & Physiology','Pregnancy Physiology','easy','flashcard','Summarise the respiratory changes in normal pregnancy.','["Minute ventilation: +40% (primarily via increased tidal volume, not respiratory rate). Tidal volume: +40%. Respiratory rate: unchanged. FRC: -20% (diaphragm elevated by gravid uterus). PaCO2: decreases to ~30 mmHg (respiratory alkalosis). PaO2: increases slightly. Serum HCO3-: compensatorily reduced (renal compensation). pH: slightly alkaline (~7.44). Diaphragmatic excursion: increased (compensates for reduced FRC)."]','A','The hypocapnia of pregnancy (low PaCO2 ~30 mmHg) creates a favourable CO2 gradient for transfer of fetal CO2 across the placenta to the mother. Progesterone stimulates the respiratory centre directly, increasing CO2 sensitivity — this is the primary driver of increased minute ventilation in pregnancy. During labour, hyperventilation in first stage can cause maternal hypocapnia → lightheadedness → symptomatic relief requires rebreathing.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Anatomy & Physiology','Pregnancy Physiology','medium','flashcard','Describe the renal changes in normal pregnancy.','["GFR: +40–65% (hyperfiltration). Renal plasma flow: +70–80% (peaks at 16 weeks then falls slightly). Serum creatinine: falls to <0.8 mg/dL (a creatinine >1.0 mg/dL is abnormal in pregnancy). Serum urea/BUN: falls. Serum urate: falls in first trimester but rises in third trimester. Glycosuria: common at normal blood glucose (tubular glucose reabsorption capacity exceeded). Ureters: dilate (progesterone → smooth muscle relaxation + right ureteric compression by gravid uterus → right-sided hydronephrosis common). Urinary frequency: first trimester (bladder compression)."]','A','Clinical implications: creatinine and urea thresholds for renal impairment must be adjusted downward in pregnancy. Asymptomatic bacteriuria is treated in pregnancy (10% progress to pyelonephritis due to urinary stasis, impaired ureteral peristalsis, glycosuria → bacterial growth). Right pyelonephritis is more common than left (right ureteric compression). Proteinuria: ≥300 mg/24h or protein:creatinine ratio ≥30 mg/mmol (≥0.3 mg/mg) is significant (pre-eclampsia threshold).','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Anatomy & Physiology','Pregnancy Physiology','medium','flashcard','Describe the haematological changes in normal pregnancy.','["Plasma volume: +40–50% (dilutional anaemia). RBC mass: +20–30% (EPO-driven erythropoiesis). Haematocrit/Hb: falls — physiological anaemia (Hb as low as 105 g/L normal). Platelets: slight fall (gestational thrombocytopenia). WBC: leukocytosis (up to 12–15 ×10⁹/L — normal in pregnancy). ESR: markedly elevated (not useful in pregnancy). Fibrinogen: +50%. Factors VII, VIII, X, XII, vWF: increase. Protein S: decreases. APC resistance: develops (acquired). → Net result: hypercoagulable state."]','A','Iron requirement in pregnancy: ~1000 mg total (fetal/placental 350 mg, expanded red cell mass 450 mg, delivery losses 200 mg). Routine iron supplementation recommended if Hb <105 g/L or if dietary intake inadequate. Folate: 400 μg/day pre-conceptionally and until 12 weeks (reduces neural tube defects); 5 mg/day if high risk (previous NTD, anti-epileptics, coeliac disease). Folic acid must be taken BEFORE conception as neural tube closes by day 28 (before most women know they are pregnant).','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Anatomy & Physiology','Pregnancy Physiology','hard','flashcard','Explain gestational diabetes mellitus (GDM) — pathophysiology, screening, and complications.','["GDM: glucose intolerance first diagnosed in pregnancy. Pathophysiology: progressive insulin resistance from mid-pregnancy (driven by hPL, cortisol, progesterone, oestrogen, prolactin) overwhelms beta-cell reserve → hyperglycaemia. Risk factors: obesity, PCOS, previous GDM, family history of T2DM, macrosomic previous baby, glucocorticoid use. Screening: OGTT (75 g) at 24–28 weeks; diagnose if fasting ≥5.1 mmol/L, 1-hour ≥10.0, or 2-hour ≥8.5 mmol/L (WHO 2013 criteria)."]','A','Fetal and maternal complications of GDM: Fetal — macrosomia (shoulder dystocia, birth injury), neonatal hypoglycaemia (fetal hyperinsulinaemia from maternal hyperglycaemia), polycythaemia, jaundice, respiratory distress, increased stillbirth risk. Maternal — increased pre-eclampsia risk, CS rate, operative delivery risk, 50% lifetime risk of T2DM. Management: diet first; if glycaemia not controlled → metformin and/or insulin. Monitoring with fasting and postprandial glucose targets.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Anatomy & Physiology','Pregnancy Physiology','medium','flashcard','What happens to thyroid function in normal pregnancy?','["TSH: falls in first trimester (hCG has weak TSH agonist activity, stimulating thyroid → negative feedback on TSH). Returns to normal in second/third trimester. Total T4/T3: increase (TBG rises 2-3× from hepatic stimulation by oestrogen → more bound hormone). Free T4/T3: remain in or near normal range. Thyroid gland: enlarges slightly (20%) due to increased iodine clearance and mild hCG stimulation. Iodine requirement: increases (recommend supplementation in iodine-deficient regions)."]','A','Clinical importance: TSH reference ranges must be adjusted in pregnancy (first trimester TSH normal lower limit 0.1, upper limit ~2.5 mIU/L per most guidelines). Gestational transient thyrotoxicosis: very high hCG (hyperemesis gravidarum, molar pregnancy) can cause mild biochemical thyrotoxicosis (low TSH, mildly elevated free T4) — usually self-limiting, no antithyroid drugs needed. Hypothyroidism in pregnancy: levothyroxine dose typically needs to increase 25–50% in early pregnancy; undertreated hypothyroidism impairs fetal neurodevelopment.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Anatomy & Physiology','Pregnancy Physiology','easy','flashcard','What gastrointestinal changes occur in normal pregnancy?','["Nausea and vomiting: first trimester (peak 6–12 weeks) — driven by hCG (positive correlation with hCG levels). Gastro-oesophageal reflux: progesterone relaxes lower oesophageal sphincter; elevated intragastric pressure from displaced stomach. Constipation: progesterone reduces gut motility (increased transit time); displacement of colon; iron supplementation. Gallstones: oestrogen increases cholesterol saturation of bile; progesterone reduces gallbladder motility (bile stasis) → 2× risk of gallstone formation in pregnancy. Delayed gastric emptying → aspiration risk during anaesthesia."]','A','Hyperemesis gravidarum (HG): severe vomiting causing >5% weight loss, dehydration, ketonuria, electrolyte disturbance (hypokalaemia, hyponatraemia). Admission, IV fluids, antiemetics (ondansetron, metoclopramide, cyclizine, promethazine), thiamine supplementation (Wernicke encephalopathy risk). Highest hCG levels (molar pregnancy, multiple pregnancy, trisomy 21) are associated with more severe HG. Ondansetron — recent data suggests slightly increased cleft palate risk if used in first trimester.','universal',false,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Anatomy & Physiology','Pregnancy Physiology','hard','flashcard','Describe the immunological adaptations in pregnancy that prevent maternal rejection of the semi-allogeneic fetus.','["(1) HLA class Ia (HLA-A, B) downregulated on syncytiotrophoblast (avoids maternal T-cell killing). (2) HLA-G and HLA-E expressed on extravillous trophoblast — inhibit NK cell lysis (inhibitory KIR receptors on dNK cells recognise HLA-G/E). (3) Regulatory T cells (Tregs) expanded by progesterone and LIF → suppress maternal immune response. (4) Complement regulatory proteins (CD55/DAF, CD59) on trophoblast prevent complement activation. (5) PD-L1 (programmed death ligand-1) on trophoblast inhibits maternal T cells."]','A','Antiphospholipid syndrome (APS): autoimmune thrombophilia associated with recurrent miscarriage (especially second trimester losses), thrombosis, and thrombocytopenia. Antibodies: anticardiolipin IgG/IgM, anti-β2-glycoprotein-I IgG/IgM, lupus anticoagulant. Mechanism: antibodies activate endothelium and platelets, inhibit trophoblast invasion, complement activation. Treatment in pregnancy: low-molecular-weight heparin + low-dose aspirin from conception. Understanding the failure of immune tolerance at the placental interface helps explain autoimmune causes of recurrent miscarriage.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Anatomy & Physiology','Pregnancy Physiology','medium','flashcard','What are the common musculoskeletal changes in pregnancy?','["Relaxin and progesterone → ligament laxity. Pubic symphysis widening (up to 10 mm, normal <9 mm). Pelvic girdle pain (PGP): relaxin-related sacroiliac and pubic symphysis joint instability. Lumbar lordosis: increases to shift centre of gravity. Carpal tunnel syndrome: fluid retention compresses median nerve at carpal tunnel (affects 25% of pregnant women). Diastasis recti: separation of rectus abdominis. Joint laxity increases risk of sprains."]','A','Pelvic girdle pain (PGP) affects ~25% of pregnant women and can be debilitating. Risk factors: previous pelvic/low back pain, previous pregnancy with PGP, high BMI, strenuous work. Management: physiotherapy (pelvic stabilisation exercises), pelvic support belt, activity modification, crutches in severe cases. Carpal tunnel syndrome is treated conservatively (wrist splints, especially at night); resolves in most cases postpartum as fluid retention resolves.','universal',false,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Anatomy & Physiology','Pregnancy Physiology','hard','flashcard','What are the physiological changes in the breast during pregnancy?','["First trimester: ductal and lobular proliferation driven by oestrogen (ductal) and progesterone (alveolar). Breast engorgement and tenderness. Nipple darkening (MSH/oestrogen). Montgomery glands hypertrophy. Second/third trimester: further alveolar development driven by prolactin, progesterone, placental lactogen, and insulin. Colostrum produced but not secreted (blocked by high oestrogen/progesterone). Breasts enlarge by 2 cup sizes on average. Areola darkens and secondary areola develops."]','A','Breast changes are often the earliest symptom of pregnancy (from ~4 weeks). The Montgomery glands (modified sebaceous glands on the areola) secrete lubricating, antimicrobial substances that protect the nipple during breastfeeding. Striae gravidarum (stretch marks) develop as breast size increases rapidly. After delivery, the fall in oestrogen/progesterone unmasks prolactin action → colostrum secretion → transitional milk (days 5–14) → mature milk (week 2 onward).','universal',false,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Anatomy & Physiology','Parturition','easy','flashcard','List the hormonal triggers of parturition (labour initiation) at term.','["(1) Oestrogen:progesterone ratio increases (rising oestrogen, functional progesterone withdrawal). (2) Oxytocin receptors upregulate 200-fold on myometrium (oestrogen-driven). (3) Prostaglandins (PGE2, PGF2α) increase in amnion, decidua — promote contractions and cervical ripening. (4) Gap junctions increase in myometrium (allowing synchronised contractions). (5) Fetal cortisol rises → stimulates placental CYP17A1 → more oestrogen. (6) CRH rises exponentially near term → amplifies prostaglandin production."]','A','The CRH clock hypothesis: placental CRH rises exponentially from ~20 weeks; the rate of rise predicts timing of labour onset. Women with highest CRH levels at 20 weeks deliver earliest (preterm); those with lowest CRH deliver latest (post-term). CRH also stimulates fetal adrenal DHEA-S production (more oestrogen precursor), creating a positive feedback amplification loop near term.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Anatomy & Physiology','Parturition','easy','flashcard','Describe the three stages of labour with their normal durations.','["First stage: onset of regular contractions to full cervical dilation (10 cm). Latent phase (0–6 cm): up to 20 hours in nullipara, 14 hours in multipara. Active phase (6–10 cm): 1 cm/hour in nullipara, 1.5–2 cm/hour in multipara (WHO 2018: active phase begins at 5 cm). Second stage: full dilation to delivery of baby. Up to 3 hours in nullipara (2 hours without epidural), 2 hours in multipara. Third stage: delivery of baby to delivery of placenta. Up to 30 minutes. Fourth stage: first 2 hours postpartum — monitoring for haemorrhage."]','A','Active management of third stage: oxytocin 10 IU IM after delivery of anterior shoulder (or within 1 minute of birth) → reduces PPH risk by 60%. Components: uterotonic drug, controlled cord traction, uterine massage. Expected blood loss: <500 mL (vaginal delivery), <1000 mL (CS). PPH defined as >500 mL (vaginal) or >1000 mL (CS). Major PPH: >1000 mL. The ''4 Ts'' of PPH: Tone (uterine atony — 80%), Tissue (retained placenta), Trauma (laceration), Thrombin (coagulopathy).','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Anatomy & Physiology','Parturition','medium','flashcard','Explain the mechanisms of action of drugs used for cervical ripening and labour induction.','["PGE2 (dinoprostone): cervical gel or pessary; binds EP3 receptors on cervical fibroblasts → MMP activation → collagen dissolution + increased hydration → cervical ripening; also stimulates myometrial EP1/EP3 receptors. Misoprostol (PGE1): sublingual, oral, or vaginal; potent uterotonic + cervical ripening — cheaper, heat-stable, increasingly used for induction. Mechanical: Foley catheter (balloon in os) → mechanical stretch stimulating prostaglandin release. Oxytocin IV: binds Gq receptors → IP3 → intracellular Ca2+ → myosin light chain kinase → contractions."]','A','Contraindications to induction: placenta praevia, vasa praevia, transverse lie, cord presentation, previous classical CS. Caution with previous CS (VBAC — risk of uterine rupture; oxytocin acceptable, prostaglandins generally avoided). Uterine hyperstimulation (>5 contractions per 10 min) → fetal hypoxia (reduced uterine blood flow during contractions) → manage by stopping oxytocin, tocolysis (terbutaline 0.25 mg SC), fetal monitoring.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Anatomy & Physiology','Parturition','medium','flashcard','What are tocolytics and when are they used?','["Tocolytics: agents that suppress uterine contractions. Used in preterm labour (PTL) to delay delivery for 48 hours to allow: antenatal corticosteroids (betamethasone/dexamethasone — fetal lung maturation), magnesium sulphate neuroprotection (if <32 weeks), and maternal transfer to tertiary centre. Types: (1) Atosiban (oxytocin receptor antagonist) — first choice in UK. (2) Nifedipine (calcium channel blocker) — widely used. (3) Indomethacin (COX inhibitor) — use <32 weeks (risk of PDA closure). (4) Betamimetics (ritodrine, salbutamol) — β2 agonists, significant cardiac side effects."]','A','Antenatal corticosteroids: betamethasone 12 mg IM × 2 doses (24 hours apart) given 24 hours–7 days before expected preterm delivery if 24–34+6 weeks. Reduces: RDS by 40%, IVH by 50%, NEC, neonatal death. A single rescue course may be given if >7 days from first course and still at risk. Magnesium sulphate (4 g IV loading dose then 1 g/hour × 24 hours) for neuroprotection if <32 weeks → reduces cerebral palsy by 30%.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Anatomy & Physiology','Parturition','hard','flashcard','Describe the physiology of epidural analgesia for labour pain.','["Epidural catheter inserted at L3–L4 interspace into the epidural space. Local anaesthetic (e.g. bupivacaine 0.1%) + opioid (fentanyl) injected → blocks sensory nerve roots as they pass through the epidural space. Effect: blocks T10–L1 (first stage pain — uterine contractions) and S2–S4 (second stage pain — perineal stretch). Motor blockade is minimised by using low-concentration local anaesthetic. CSF is not entered (unlike spinal), so no post-dural puncture headache risk if correctly sited."]','A','Side effects of epidural: hypotension (most common — treat with IV fluid, left lateral tilt, vasopressors if necessary); pyrexia (epidural fever — not infective); motor block (immobility, increased operative delivery rate); urinary retention (catheter often needed); itching (from opioids — treat with naloxone or ondansetron); patchy or failed block; dural puncture headache (1% — post-dural puncture headache, treated with blood patch). Contraindications: patient refusal, coagulopathy, thrombocytopenia <70, local infection, raised ICP.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Anatomy & Physiology','Parturition','medium','flashcard','What is shoulder dystocia and what manoeuvres are used?','["Shoulder dystocia: failure of the fetal shoulder to deliver spontaneously after delivery of the head (anterior shoulder impacted behind the pubic symphysis). Rate: 0.2–3% of vaginal deliveries. Risk factors: macrosomia, GDM, prolonged second stage, previous shoulder dystocia, obesity. Manoeuvres (HELPERR mnemonic): H-Call for Help; E-Evaluate for Episiotomy; L-Legs (McRoberts position — hyperflexion of maternal thighs to increase AP outlet diameter); P-Suprapubic Pressure; E-Enter (internal manoeuvres — Rubin II, Woods screw); R-Remove the posterior arm; R-Roll to all-fours (Gaskin)."]','A','McRoberts position (the first internal manoeuvre tried) flattens the lumbar lordosis and rotates the pubic symphysis superiorly, increasing the functional AP diameter of the pelvis. It alone resolves ~40% of cases. Suprapubic pressure (Mazzanti) is applied simultaneously to dislodge the anterior shoulder. Fundal pressure (Kristeller) is CONTRAINDICATED — worsens impaction. Complication: fetal brachial plexus injury (Erb''s palsy — C5/6, ''waiter''s tip'' deformity), clavicle fracture, hypoxic brain injury.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Anatomy & Physiology','Parturition','easy','flashcard','What are the main uterotonics used to prevent and treat postpartum haemorrhage?','["(1) Oxytocin (Syntocinon): first-line; IV infusion or IM; stimulates myometrial Gq receptors → Ca2+ → contractions. (2) Ergometrine: ergot alkaloid; stimulates myometrial smooth muscle directly; potent and prolonged; avoid in hypertension (vasoconstriction). (3) Syntometrine: oxytocin + ergometrine combined IM injection — used in active management of third stage. (4) Carboprost (15-methyl PGF2α): IM or intramyometrial; potent; contraindicated in asthma. (5) Misoprostol (PGE1): sublingual/rectal; used in resource-limited settings (heat-stable, no refrigeration needed). (6) Tranexamic acid: antifibrinolytic — reduces mortality in PPH if given within 3 hours."]','A','WOMAN trial (2017): tranexamic acid given early in PPH (within 3 hours of delivery) reduces death from haemorrhage by 30% with no thromboembolic risk. WHO now recommends it as standard treatment for PPH. The ''4 Ts'' of PPH aetiology guide treatment: Tone (atony) → uterotonic drugs, bimanual uterine compression, aortic compression, surgical (uterine balloon tamponade, B-Lynch suture, uterine artery ligation, hysterectomy). Tissue (retained) → evacuation. Trauma → repair. Thrombin → FFP, cryoprecipitate, platelets.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Anatomy & Physiology','Parturition','hard','flashcard','Describe preterm birth — definition, aetiology, and pathophysiological mechanisms.','["Definition: birth before 37+0 weeks (extreme <28 weeks; very preterm 28–31+6 weeks; moderate to late preterm 32–36+6 weeks). Prevalence: ~8–12% globally. Aetiologies: (1) Infection/inflammation (~40%): ascending infection (BV, chorioamnionitis) → prostaglandin and cytokine production → preterm labour. (2) Uterine overdistension: multiple pregnancy, polyhydramnios. (3) Cervical insufficiency: painless dilation without contractions. (4) Uteroplacental ischaemia: abruption, pre-eclampsia. (5) Iatrogenic: indicated preterm delivery for maternal/fetal compromise."]','A','Prevention strategies: (1) Cervical length screening at 19–24 weeks (transvaginal ultrasound) — cervical length <25 mm at risk. (2) Progesterone (vaginal, 200 mg/day): reduces PTB in singleton with short cervix (<25 mm) by ~45%. (3) Cervical cerclage: for cervical insufficiency or short cervix with previous PTB. (4) Screening and treatment of asymptomatic BV (debated). Neonatal complications: RDS (surfactant deficiency), IVH, necrotising enterocolitis (NEC), retinopathy of prematurity, chronic lung disease. Each week of gestation after 24 weeks significantly improves survival outcomes.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Anatomy & Physiology','Parturition','hard','flashcard','Explain the pathophysiology of uterine atony and its management.','["Uterine atony: failure of the myometrium to contract and retract after delivery, causing haemorrhage from the placental bed (open sinusoids). Most common cause of PPH (80%). Risk factors: overdistended uterus (polyhydramnios, macrosomia, multiple pregnancy), prolonged labour (uterine fatigue), grand multiparity, chorioamnionitis, general anaesthesia (volatile agents are uterine relaxants). Pathophysiology: failure of the ''living ligation'' mechanism (myometrial fibres do not compress intramyometrial vessels)."]','A','Surgical management of refractory atony: (1) Uterine balloon tamponade (Bakri balloon — inflated in uterine cavity; 90% success). (2) B-Lynch suture: compressive suture around the uterus. (3) Bilateral uterine artery ligation. (4) Bilateral internal iliac artery ligation (reduces pulse pressure to uterus by 85%). (5) Interventional radiology: uterine artery embolisation (if haemodynamically stable). (6) Hysterectomy: definitive — peripartum hysterectomy carries significant morbidity. Step-up approach is preferred, escalating from conservative to surgical.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Anatomy & Physiology','Parturition','medium','flashcard','What are the cardinal movements of the fetal head during vaginal delivery?','["(1) Engagement: biparietal diameter (BPD) descends below the pelvic inlet. (2) Descent: continuous throughout labour. (3) Flexion: fetal chin approaches chest → smaller diameter presented. (4) Internal rotation: occiput rotates to anterior (occiput anterior — OA) at the level of the ischial spines (against the levator ani). (5) Extension: fetal head extends as it passes beneath the pubic arch → head born. (6) Restitution: head rotates back to its natural alignment with the shoulders. (7) External rotation: shoulders rotate to anteroposterior position. (8) Expulsion: anterior then posterior shoulder delivered."]','A','Malpositions (not malpresentations): occiput posterior (OP) — fetal occiput faces maternal sacrum. Causes prolonged labour (less favourable flexion), backache, more maternal pushing. Often rotates spontaneously; if not → manual rotation, Kjelland''s rotational forceps, or CS. Transverse arrest (occiput transverse, OT): may not rotate spontaneously. Face presentation: extension of fetal neck (mento-anterior can deliver vaginally; mento-posterior cannot). Brow presentation: usually requires CS.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Anatomy & Physiology','Lactation & Postpartum','easy','flashcard','Describe the hormonal control of milk production (lactogenesis) and milk ejection.','["Lactogenesis I (mid-pregnancy): alveolar cells differentiate into secretory cells under prolactin, cortisol, insulin — but milk secretion is blocked by high oestrogen/progesterone. Lactogenesis II (48–72 hours postpartum): fall of placental oestrogen and progesterone removes inhibition → prolactin acts → copious milk production begins. Lactogenesis III (galactopoiesis — ongoing): maintained by suckling → prolactin surges. Milk ejection (let-down): suckling → oxytocin release from posterior pituitary → myoepithelial cell contraction → milk ejected through ducts."]','A','Prolactin surge with suckling: each feeding episode causes a prolactin surge lasting 45–60 minutes. Frequent suckling maintains high prolactin levels. Without suckling, prolactin returns to normal within weeks → lactation ceases (involution). Failure of lactogenesis II (failure of milk ''coming in'' by day 4): consider retained placental fragment (continuing progesterone production), Sheehan syndrome (anterior pituitary necrosis), primary lactation failure.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Anatomy & Physiology','Lactation & Postpartum','easy','flashcard','Compare colostrum, transitional milk, and mature breast milk.','["Colostrum (days 1–5): thick, yellow; high IgA (50–100× more than mature milk), protein (~87 g/L), leukocytes, growth factors, lactoferrin; lower fat and lactose. Volume: 3–5 mL/feed initially (matches newborn stomach size ~5–7 mL). Transitional milk (days 5–14): gradual shift toward mature milk composition. Mature milk (from ~2 weeks): white; protein ~9 g/L; fat ~35 g/L (varies: foremilk low-fat, hindmilk high-fat); lactose ~70 g/L; volume 750–850 mL/day."]','A','Benefits of breastfeeding for infant: reduces SIDS risk (50%); protects against respiratory tract infections, otitis media, gastroenteritis (secretory IgA coats mucosal surfaces); reduces atopic disease; reduces obesity and T1DM; promotes jaw/dental development; optimal neurological development (DHA in breast milk). Benefits for mother: reduces breast and ovarian cancer risk (dose-dependent with months breastfed); accelerates uterine involution (oxytocin); aids weight loss (500 kcal/day extra expenditure); contraceptive effect (LAM).','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Anatomy & Physiology','Lactation & Postpartum','medium','flashcard','What is the Lactational Amenorrhoea Method (LAM) and how does it work?','["LAM: natural contraceptive method based on breastfeeding-induced anovulation. Three conditions required: (1) Fully or nearly fully breastfeeding (no supplementary feeds, feeds day and night, <6 hours between feeds). (2) Infant age <6 months. (3) Maternal amenorrhoea (no menstrual period since delivery). When all 3 criteria met: efficacy ~98% (comparable to hormonal contraception). Mechanism: frequent suckling → high prolactin → suppresses pulsatile GnRH → low FSH/LH → anovulation → amenorrhoea."]','A','Practical advice: When any criterion fails (infant >6 months, periods return, supplementary feeds introduced), an additional contraceptive method is needed. GnRH suppression from breastfeeding is dose-dependent on suckling frequency and duration — formula supplementation (even one feed) significantly reduces the contraceptive effect. LAM is widely used in Africa and the developing world where it makes a meaningful contribution to child spacing and maternal health.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Anatomy & Physiology','Lactation & Postpartum','medium','flashcard','What are the normal postpartum physiological changes in the first 6 weeks?','["Uterine involution: complete by 6 weeks (1 kg → 60–80 g). Lochia: rubra (red/bloody, days 1–4) → serosa (pink-brown, days 4–10) → alba (white/yellow, weeks 2–6). Hormonal changes: oestrogen and progesterone remain low while breastfeeding. TSH rises and normalises. Return of menstruation: non-breastfeeding women — ovulation at ~6 weeks, menstruation at ~8–10 weeks. Breastfeeding women — delayed (weeks to months). Cervical changes: external os regains slit-like shape. Vaginal atrophy: low oestrogen → dryness, dyspareunia."]','A','Postnatal check (6 weeks): assess wound healing (CS/perineal), blood pressure (hypertension may persist post-preeclampsia), urinary/faecal incontinence, pelvic floor function, mental health (Edinburgh Postnatal Depression Scale — score ≥13 indicates likely depression; 10–15% of women). Contraception: COCP contraindicated while fully breastfeeding (oestrogen reduces milk production); progesterone-only pill (POP) is safe from day 21; LARC (IUD, implant) from 4 weeks.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Anatomy & Physiology','Lactation & Postpartum','hard','flashcard','Describe the pathophysiology and management of postpartum depression (PPD).','["PPD: depressive episode beginning within 4 weeks (DSM) to 1 year (NICE) postpartum. Prevalence: 10–15% of women. Pathophysiology: rapid postpartum fall in oestrogen and progesterone (which have GABAergic and serotonergic modulating effects) → vulnerability in predisposed women. GABA-A receptor hypersensitivity (↑GABAAR-containing α subunits) may underlie PPD — targeted by brexanolone (IV neuroactive steroid/GABA modulator, FDA-approved 2019). Risk factors: previous depression/anxiety, lack of social support, domestic violence, unwanted pregnancy."]','A','Brexanolone (Zulresso): IV neuroactive steroid (GABAAR positive allosteric modulator, synthetic analogue of allopregnanolone — the neurosteroid metabolite of progesterone) approved for moderate-severe PPD. Administered as 60-hour IV infusion. Rapid onset (within 24 hours). Mechanism: restores GABAergic inhibitory tone that falls with post-delivery progesterone withdrawal. SSRIs (sertraline first-line, compatible with breastfeeding) remain standard treatment. PPD must be distinguished from ''baby blues'' (days 3–5, self-limiting, affects 50–80%) and puerperal psychosis (rare, 1/1000, psychotic features — psychiatric emergency).','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Anatomy & Physiology','Lactation & Postpartum','medium','flashcard','What is galactorrhoea and what are its common causes?','["Galactorrhoea: spontaneous milk flow from the breast not associated with breastfeeding (>6 months after stopping breastfeeding or in non-postpartum women). Causes: Hyperprolactinaemia (most common cause): prolactinoma (most common pituitary tumour), hypothyroidism (TRH stimulates prolactin), dopamine antagonist drugs (antipsychotics, metoclopramide, domperidone, haloperidol), renal failure (reduced prolactin clearance), chest wall stimulation. Non-hyperprolactinaemia: idiopathic, pregnancy. Evaluation: prolactin level, thyroid function, MRI pituitary if macro-adenoma suspected."]','A','Prolactinoma management: Medical — dopamine agonists (cabergoline preferred over bromocriptine — fewer side effects, once/twice weekly dosing). Cabergoline normalises prolactin and shrinks tumour in 90% of microadenomas. Monitor: MRI at 6 months initially. Surgery (trans-sphenoidal): if resistant to medical therapy or vision threatened by suprasellar extension. In pregnancy with prolactinoma: cabergoline stopped once pregnancy confirmed (microadenomas rarely grow); monitor for visual field changes (tumour may expand). Macroprolactinaemia (IgG-bound prolactin): high prolactin but no symptoms or tumour — bioassay inactive.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Anatomy & Physiology','Lactation & Postpartum','easy','flashcard','What are the contraindications to breastfeeding?','["Absolute contraindications: Maternal HIV infection (in resource-rich settings with safe formula available — WHO still recommends breastfeeding in resource-limited settings with ARV coverage). Active untreated TB (pulmonary — airborne, not milk-borne — isolate mother; breastfeeding can continue with expressing). HTLV-1 infection. Active herpes simplex lesion on the breast. Infant with galactosaemia (cannot process lactose/galactose). Certain maternal medications: radioactive iodine (I-131), cytotoxic chemotherapy, lithium, retinoids (isotretinoin), ergotamine."]','A','Drug safety in breastfeeding: most medications are compatible (infant receives <1% of maternal dose via milk). Safe: paracetamol, NSAIDs (ibuprofen preferred over aspirin — aspirin → Reye''s syndrome risk), amoxicillin, flucloxacillin, metronidazole, omeprazole, sertraline, nifedipine, atenolol (small amounts). Avoid: lithium (renal toxicity), tetracyclines (bone/tooth deposition), combined OCP (reduces milk supply), antithyroid carbimazole (preferred over propylthiouracil in breastfeeding). The LactMed database (NIH) is a comprehensive resource.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Anatomy & Physiology','Lactation & Postpartum','hard','flashcard','Explain the concept of maternal hypothyroidism postpartum (postpartum thyroiditis).','["Postpartum thyroiditis (PPT): autoimmune thyroid disease occurring within 12 months of delivery. Affects ~5–10% of women. Typically follows a triphasic course: (1) Hyperthyroid phase (1–4 months): thyroid destruction releases preformed T4/T3 → thyrotoxicosis (palpitations, anxiety, heat intolerance). TSH suppressed. (2) Hypothyroid phase (4–8 months): depleted thyroid stores → hypothyroidism (fatigue, cold intolerance, depression). TSH elevated. (3) Recovery (most resolve by 12 months; 25% develop permanent hypothyroidism)."]','A','Risk factors for PPT: anti-TPO antibody positivity in first trimester (50% of positive women develop PPT), personal/family history of autoimmune thyroid disease, type 1 diabetes. Management: Hyper phase — symptomatic (beta-blocker if needed); no antithyroid drugs (destructive thyroiditis, not synthesis excess). Hypo phase — levothyroxine if symptomatic or breastfeeding (T4 crosses into milk in minimal amounts; maternal hypothyroidism must be corrected). Must be distinguished from Graves'' disease relapse (TSH receptor antibodies positive, radioiodine uptake elevated vs suppressed in PPT).','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Anatomy & Physiology','Lactation & Postpartum','medium','flashcard','What are the risk factors and clinical features of postpartum venous thromboembolism (VTE)?','["VTE is 5–10× more common in the postpartum period (especially first 6 weeks). Risk factors: CS (2× VTE risk vs vaginal delivery), obesity (BMI>30), prolonged immobility, pre-eclampsia, PPH requiring transfusion, thrombophilia (Factor V Leiden, protein C/S deficiency, antiphospholipid syndrome), age >35, multiple pregnancy, paraplegia. Pathophysiology: hypercoagulable state persists postpartum + venous stasis (uterine compression gone but venous tone still low) + endothelial injury (delivery)."]','A','Thromboprophylaxis: all women should receive risk assessment at booking and postpartum. High-risk women (e.g. previous VTE, thrombophilia) receive LMWH antenatally and for 6 weeks postpartum. CS patients receive LMWH for 10 days (standard risk) or 6 weeks (high risk). Compression stockings for moderate risk. LMWH is safe in breastfeeding (negligible oral bioavailability, not significantly absorbed by infant). RCOG Green-top Guideline 37a/b covers thromboprophylaxis in pregnancy and puerperium.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Anatomy & Physiology','Lactation & Postpartum','hard','flashcard','Describe Sheehan''s syndrome — mechanism, presentation, and management.','["Mechanism: massive postpartum haemorrhage → severe hypotension → vasospasm of pituitary blood supply → ischaemic necrosis of anterior pituitary (which is hypertrophied in pregnancy, increasing metabolic demand). The pituitary gland doubles in size during pregnancy (primarily lactotrophs). Posterior pituitary usually spared (different blood supply). Hormonal deficiencies in decreasing frequency: GH (most commonly lost first) → LH/FSH (amenorrhoea) → TSH (hypothyroidism) → ACTH (adrenal insufficiency) → Prolactin (failure of lactation — classically the first presenting symptom)."]','A','Diagnosis: clinical (failure to lactate after delivery → should prompt investigation) + basal hormones (low FT4 with low/normal TSH, low LH/FSH with low oestradiol, low IGF-1, low morning cortisol with failure to respond to Synacthen test) + MRI pituitary (empty sella sign — fibrotic remnant of necrotic gland). Management: hormone replacement starting with hydrocortisone (ACTH deficiency is life-threatening), then levothyroxine (start after cortisol replaced — thyroid replacement without cortisol can precipitate Addisonian crisis), then oestrogen, growth hormone if required. Fertility possible with gonadotrophin injections (FSH/LH) for ovulation induction.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Anatomy & Physiology','Male Reproductive Physiology','easy','flashcard','Describe the anatomy of the testis and the cells within the seminiferous tubule.','["Testis: paired oval organs in the scrotum (scrotal temperature 2–4°C cooler than core → required for spermatogenesis). Contains ~900 coiled seminiferous tubules (each >0.5 m long). Cells within seminiferous tubule: Sertoli cells (''nurse cells'' — supporting spermatogenesis, form blood-testis barrier, produce inhibin B, AMH, androgen-binding protein (ABP), activin); Spermatogonia (stem cells, outermost layer); progressing inward: primary spermatocytes → secondary spermatocytes → spermatids → spermatozoa. Between tubules: Leydig cells (produce testosterone in response to LH)."]','A','Temperature regulation of the testis: Pampiniform plexus (venous plexus surrounding testicular artery in the spermatic cord) acts as a countercurrent heat exchanger — venous blood from the testis cools the arterial blood entering the testis. Cryptorchidism (undescended testis): if uncorrected before age 2, progressive seminiferous tubule damage occurs → infertility. Also 4–8× increased risk of testicular cancer (even in the contralateral descended testis). Orchidopexy (surgical fixation to scrotum) recommended before 12–18 months.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Anatomy & Physiology','Male Reproductive Physiology','easy','flashcard','Describe the stages of spermatogenesis.','["(1) Spermatogonia (2n, diploid) undergo mitosis → some self-renew, some → primary spermatocytes. (2) Primary spermatocytes (2n) enter meiosis I → secondary spermatocytes (n, haploid — each with duplicated chromatids). (3) Secondary spermatocytes rapidly undergo meiosis II → spermatids (n, haploid, single chromatids). (4) Spermiogenesis: spermatids undergo morphological transformation → mature spermatozoa (head with nucleus + acrosome; midpiece with mitochondria; tail/flagellum). Duration: 64–74 days. Mitochondria are maternal (from oocyte cytoplasm, not paternal sperm mitochondria)."]','A','Why only maternal mitochondria in offspring: during fertilisation, paternal sperm mitochondria are actively ubiquitinated and destroyed after sperm entry into the oocyte. All mitochondrial DNA is therefore maternally inherited. Exceptions: rare cases of paternal mtDNA leakage cause ''paternal'' mitochondrial inheritance. This is clinically relevant in mitochondrial DNA diseases (Leber optic neuropathy, MELAS, MERRF) which follow maternal inheritance patterns. Preimplantation genetic testing (PGT-M) can screen for mtDNA mutations in IVF embryos.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Anatomy & Physiology','Male Reproductive Physiology','medium','flashcard','Describe the hypothalamic-pituitary-testicular (HPT) axis.','["GnRH (pulsatile, from arcuate nucleus) → anterior pituitary: LH + FSH. LH → Leydig cells → testosterone (and small amounts of oestradiol via aromatase). FSH → Sertoli cells → inhibin B + ABP (concentrates testosterone in tubule) → supports spermatogenesis. Negative feedback: Testosterone → hypothalamus + pituitary (inhibits GnRH pulses and LH). Inhibin B → pituitary (selectively inhibits FSH). DHT (more potent) also exerts negative feedback."]','A','Clinical application of the HPT axis: Hypogonadotrophic hypogonadism (low LH, low FSH, low testosterone): Kallmann syndrome, pituitary tumour, haemochromatosis, anabolic steroid use → pulsatile GnRH pump or gonadotrophin injections for fertility. Hypergonadotrophic hypogonadism (high LH, high FSH, low testosterone): Klinefelter (47,XXY), severe testicular failure — testosterone replacement for virilisation but gonadotrophins needed for fertility (TESE). Anabolic steroid abuse → suppresses HPT axis → testicular atrophy, azoospermia (reversible after cessation in most cases).','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Anatomy & Physiology','Male Reproductive Physiology','medium','flashcard','What are the physiological effects of testosterone?','["Fetal: virilisation of Wolffian ducts (epididymis, vas, seminal vesicles). Descent of testes (with DHT). Pubertal: testicular growth, spermatogenesis (via Sertoli cells — intratesticular testosterone 100× higher than serum). Virilisation: laryngeal growth (voice deepening), pubic/axillary/facial hair, acne. Muscle mass increase (anabolic). Linear bone growth (GH synergy), epiphyseal closure. Adult: libido, erectile function (central and peripheral effects), erythropoiesis (raises Hb ~1 g/dL), mood, cognitive function."]','A','DHT (from testosterone via 5α-reductase type 2) mediates: external genitalia development in fetal life, prostate growth, male pattern baldness (via miniaturisation of scalp hair follicles), acne (sebaceous gland stimulation). Finasteride (5α-reductase type 2 inhibitor) blocks DHT: used for BPH (reduces prostate volume) and androgenetic alopecia. Dutasteride blocks type 1 and 2 — more effective for BPH. Do not use in pregnancy (causes feminisation of male fetus). Testosterone to oestradiol conversion via aromatase (in fat, liver, brain): clinically relevant in obese men (excess oestrogens from aromatisation → gynaecomastia, suppressed spermatogenesis).','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Anatomy & Physiology','Male Reproductive Physiology','hard','flashcard','Describe male infertility — causes, investigation, and management.','["Male factor contributes to 40–50% of infertility. Causes: (1) Pretesticular (hypogonadotrophic): Kallmann syndrome, hyperprolactinaemia, anabolic steroids. (2) Testicular (hypergonadotrophic): Klinefelter (47,XXY), Y chromosome microdeletion (AZF region), cryptorchidism, orchitis (mumps), chemotherapy/radiotherapy, varicocele. (3) Post-testicular (obstructive azoospermia): CBAVD (CF mutations), epididymal obstruction, vasectomy. Investigation: semen analysis (WHO 2021: volume ≥1.4 mL, concentration ≥16×10⁶/mL, total motility ≥42%, morphology ≥4% normal forms)."]','A','Y chromosome microdeletions: AZFa deletion (Sertoli-cell-only syndrome — complete absence of germ cells), AZFb deletion (maturation arrest at spermatocyte stage), AZFc deletion (most common — variable sperm production, may have some sperm on TESE). AZFc deletions are paternally transmitted — sons will also have the deletion and likely be infertile (relevant genetic counselling point). Sperm retrieval techniques: PESA (percutaneous epididymal sperm aspiration), TESA/TESE (testicular sperm extraction), mTESE (microsurgical TESE — highest yield in non-obstructive azoospermia).','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Anatomy & Physiology','Male Reproductive Physiology','medium','flashcard','What is a varicocele and how does it impair fertility?','["Varicocele: dilatation of the pampiniform plexus of testicular veins within the spermatic cord. Present in ~15% of men and ~35% of men with infertility. More common on the left (left testicular vein drains to left renal vein at right angles — higher hydrostatic pressure; right drains directly to IVC at a more acute angle). Mechanism of infertility: impaired countercurrent heat exchange → elevated scrotal/testicular temperature → heat-induced germ cell apoptosis, oxidative stress, hypoxia (venous stasis), and accumulation of adrenal metabolites. Results in oligospermia, asthenospermia, and abnormal morphology."]','A','Diagnosis: clinical (grade 1: palpable only during Valsalva; grade 2: palpable at rest; grade 3: visible) + confirmed by scrotal Doppler ultrasound (retrograde venous flow during Valsalva). Treatment: varicocelectomy (microsurgical subinguinal — gold standard) or embolisation. Meta-analyses show varicocelectomy improves semen parameters and pregnancy rates in couples with clinical varicocele + abnormal semen analysis + otherwise unexplained infertility. Indications: bilateral, large, oligospermia (<15×10⁶/mL), or adolescent with growth arrest of affected testis.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Anatomy & Physiology','Male Reproductive Physiology','easy','flashcard','Describe the composition of semen and normal semen analysis parameters.','["Normal WHO 2021 semen analysis reference values (5th percentile lower reference limits): Volume ≥1.4 mL; pH ≥7.2; Total sperm count ≥39×10⁶ per ejaculate; Concentration ≥16×10⁶/mL; Total motility (PR+NP) ≥42%; Progressive motility (PR) ≥30%; Vitality (live sperm) ≥54%; Normal forms (strict Kruger morphology) ≥4%. Composition: seminal vesicles (60%): fructose, prostaglandins, semenogelin; Prostate (30%): PSA, zinc, citric acid, acid phosphatase; Bulbourethral glands (5%): mucus; Epididymal fluid (5%): sperm."]','A','Semen analysis interpretation: Oligospermia (<16×10⁶/mL); Asthenospermia (<42% motile); Teratospermia (<4% normal forms); Azoospermia (no sperm in ejaculate — obstructive vs non-obstructive). Note: WHO 2021 lowered reference values from WHO 2010 (concentration: was ≥15×10⁶/mL — similar). Always repeat abnormal semen analysis after 3 months (time for a full spermatogenic cycle). Fructose in semen confirms seminal vesicle contribution; absent fructose suggests obstruction at the level of ejaculatory duct or seminal vesicle agenesis (as in CBAVD).','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Anatomy & Physiology','Male Reproductive Physiology','hard','flashcard','What is complete androgen insensitivity syndrome (CAIS) and what is its presentation?','["CAIS (formerly testicular feminisation): 46,XY individual with loss-of-function mutation in the androgen receptor (AR) gene (X-linked). Phenotype: female external genitalia (normal labia, clitoris); blind-ending vagina (no uterus or upper vagina — no Müllerian structures because AMH was produced and was functional); absent uterus and fallopian tubes; testes present (intra-abdominal or inguinal — often presenting as inguinal hernia in infancy); normal female breast development at puberty (testosterone aromatised to oestrogen — oestrogen receptors intact); absent pubic/axillary hair (androgen-dependent); primary amenorrhoea."]','A','Hormonal profile: testosterone in high normal male range (normal Leydig cell function — but ineffective); LH elevated (loss of androgen negative feedback); oestradiol slightly elevated (aromatisation of T). Management: gonadectomy after puberty is complete (risk of gonadoblastoma/malignancy ~3–5% in retained testes; risk increases with age); oestrogen HRT after gonadectomy; vaginal dilators (Frank technique) or vaginoplasty if vagina too short; gender identity: raised female, identity female in vast majority. Genetic counselling for family members (X-linked: maternal carriers have 50% chance of affected XY offspring).','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Anatomy & Physiology','Male Reproductive Physiology','medium','flashcard','Describe the process of penile erection and the mechanism of action of PDE5 inhibitors.','["Erection: Psychic or tactile stimuli → parasympathetic activation (S2–S4 via pelvic splanchnic nerves) → release of nitric oxide (NO) from non-adrenergic non-cholinergic (NANC) neurons and vascular endothelium in erectile tissue. NO → activates soluble guanylate cyclase → increased cGMP → smooth muscle relaxation → vasodilation of helicine arteries → blood fills lacunar spaces of corpora cavernosa → compression of emissary veins → sustained erection. PDE5 (phosphodiesterase type 5) degrades cGMP → flaccidity. PDE5 inhibitors (sildenafil, tadalafil) block cGMP degradation → enhance and prolong erection."]','A','Sildenafil (Viagra): selective PDE5 inhibitor; on-demand 30–60 min before intercourse; duration ~4 hours. Tadalafil (Cialis): longer duration (~36 hours) — ''weekend pill''; also used daily for BPH. Drug interactions: nitrates are absolutely contraindicated with PDE5 inhibitors (both cause NO-mediated vasodilation → severe hypotension — potentially fatal). PDE5 inhibitors also used for pulmonary arterial hypertension (PDE5 expressed in pulmonary vasculature). Priapism (prolonged erection >4 hours): emergency — ischaemic priapism causes permanent erectile dysfunction if untreated; managed with aspiration/intracavernous phenylephrine (α1 agonist).','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Anatomy & Physiology','Male Reproductive Physiology','hard','flashcard','Describe the pathophysiology and treatment of benign prostatic hyperplasia (BPH).','["BPH: non-malignant enlargement of the transitional zone of the prostate, affecting ~50% of men by age 60 and ~80% by 80. Pathophysiology: DHT (from testosterone via 5α-reductase type 2 in prostatic stromal cells) drives prostatic stromal and glandular cell proliferation. Oestrogen may also contribute (rises relative to testosterone with age). Symptoms (LUTS): storage (frequency, nocturia, urgency) and voiding (hesitancy, poor stream, terminal dribbling, incomplete emptying). Complications: urinary retention, recurrent UTI, bladder hypertrophy, upper tract dilatation, renal impairment."]','A','Medical treatment: (1) α1-adrenergic blockers (tamsulosin, alfuzosin): relax smooth muscle in prostate stroma and bladder neck → immediate symptom relief. Side effects: orthostatic hypotension, retrograde ejaculation (intraoperative floppy iris syndrome — warn ophthalmologist before cataract surgery). (2) 5α-reductase inhibitors (finasteride, dutasteride): reduce prostate volume by ~25% over 6 months by blocking DHT; best in large prostates (>40 mL); lowers PSA by ~50% (adjust PSA interpretation). (3) Combination therapy: most effective for large prostates with severe LUTS. Surgical: TURP (gold standard); laser (HoLEP/GreenLight); prostatic urethral lift (Urolift).','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Anatomy & Physiology','Puberty & Menopause','easy','flashcard','Describe the Tanner stages of puberty in girls.','["Tanner stages in girls: Stage 1 (prepubertal): no breast or pubic hair development. Stage 2: breast bud (papilla + areola elevation); sparse pubic hair along labia (onset ~11 years). Stage 3: further breast enlargement; darker, curlier pubic hair spreading; pubic hair extends beyond labia; peak height velocity. Stage 4: secondary mound (areola and papilla form secondary mound above breast); pubic hair adult-type but not yet to medial thigh. Stage 5 (adult): breast contour smooth; pubic hair adult distribution to medial thigh. Menarche typically occurs at stage 3–4."]','A','Precocious puberty in girls: defined as any pubertal signs before age 8. Central (GnRH-dependent) — most commonly idiopathic (90% in girls); diagnose with GnRH stimulation test (LH rises >5 IU/L). Peripheral (GnRH-independent) — McCune-Albright syndrome (café-au-lait spots, fibrous dysplasia, autonomous gonadal activation), ovarian cyst/tumour, exogenous oestrogen exposure. Treatment of central PP: GnRH agonist (leuprolide depot) to suppress gonadotrophins and arrest pubertal progression, preserving adult height potential.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Anatomy & Physiology','Puberty & Menopause','easy','flashcard','Describe the Tanner stages of puberty in boys.','["Tanner stages in boys: Stage 1 (prepubertal): testicular volume <4 mL. Stage 2: testicular enlargement >4 mL (2.5 cm) — FIRST SIGN of puberty in boys (~onset 11.5 years); sparse pubic hair at base of penis. Stage 3: penile enlargement (length and breadth); darker, curlier pubic hair; voice breaking begins; early spermarche. Stage 4: further penile/scrotal growth (darker); pubic hair adult-type; axillary hair; acne; voice deepens. Stage 5 (adult): adult genital size; pubic hair to medial thigh; facial hair. Growth spurt peaks at stage 3–4 (later than girls)."]','A','Boys'' puberty typically begins 6 months later than girls (onset 11.5 vs 11 years). Delayed puberty in boys: defined as absent testicular enlargement by age 14. Most common cause: constitutional delay of growth and puberty (CDGP) — bone age delayed, positive family history, will self-resolve. Distinguish from hypogonadotrophic hypogonadism (Kallmann — anosmia) and hypergonadotrophic hypogonadism (Klinefelter). Short course of low-dose testosterone (50 mg IM monthly × 3–6 months) can stimulate puberty in CDGP.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Anatomy & Physiology','Puberty & Menopause','medium','flashcard','Explain the role of kisspeptin in puberty and reproduction.','["Kisspeptin: neuropeptide encoded by KISS1 gene, produced by two hypothalamic neuron populations: (1) Arcuate nucleus (ARC) — kisspeptin, neurokinin B, dynorphin (KNDy neurons); drive pulsatile GnRH secretion (GnRH pulse generator). (2) Anteroventral periventricular nucleus (AVPV) — responsible for the LH surge (positive feedback, oestrogen-sensitive). KISS1R (GPR54): receptor on GnRH neurons — activation triggers GnRH pulse. At puberty: KNDy neuron activity increases → more frequent/larger GnRH pulses → LH/FSH rise → gonadal steroidogenesis."]','A','Clinical relevance: Loss-of-function KISS1R mutations → hypogonadotrophic hypogonadism (delayed puberty, amenorrhoea). Gain-of-function KISS1R mutations → central precocious puberty. Kisspeptin is also involved in: negative feedback suppression of GnRH (oestrogen inhibits KNDy neurons in ARC) and positive feedback (oestrogen stimulates AVPV kisspeptin neurons → LH surge). Menopause: falling oestrogen reduces inhibition of ARC KNDy neurons → increased neurokinin B → hot flushes (neurokinin B receptor antagonists, e.g. fezolinetant, now approved for menopausal vasomotor symptoms).','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Anatomy & Physiology','Puberty & Menopause','medium','flashcard','Describe the symptoms, investigation, and management of premature ovarian insufficiency (POI).','["POI (premature menopause): loss of normal ovarian function before age 40. Affects ~1% of women under 40, 0.1% under 30. Causes: idiopathic (most common, 75%), autoimmune (Addison disease, thyroid), Turner mosaic, fragile X premutation (FMR1), iatrogenic (chemotherapy, radiotherapy, bilateral oophorectomy). Symptoms: menstrual irregularity/amenorrhoea, vasomotor symptoms, dyspareunia. Investigation: FSH >25 IU/L on two occasions ≥4 weeks apart; oestradiol low. Karyotype, autoantibodies, FMR1 permutation."]','A','Management of POI: HRT (not just contraceptives — HRT doses are higher and more physiological) until natural age of menopause (~51 years) to: relieve symptoms, prevent osteoporosis (bone density loss is accelerated), reduce cardiovascular risk, protect cognitive function. Fertility: spontaneous ovulation occurs intermittently in some women (~5% conceive spontaneously). Oocyte donation is the most effective fertility treatment for POI. Fertility preservation before anticipated POI (chemotherapy): oocyte/embryo cryopreservation or ovarian tissue cryopreservation.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Anatomy & Physiology','Puberty & Menopause','hard','flashcard','Describe the long-term consequences of oestrogen deficiency after menopause and the rationale for HRT.','["Consequences of oestrogen deficiency: (1) Vasomotor symptoms (hot flushes, night sweats) — affecting 70–80% of women. (2) Genitourinary syndrome of menopause (GSM): vaginal atrophy, dryness, dyspareunia, recurrent UTI, urinary urgency. (3) Osteoporosis: loss of 2–3% bone density/year for 5–7 years post-menopause (rapid phase). (4) Cardiovascular: loss of vasodilatory, anti-atherogenic effects of oestrogen → increased cardiovascular risk. (5) Mood/cognitive: depression, anxiety, impaired concentration. (6) Skin/hair changes: skin thinning, hair loss."]','A','HRT rationale and risks (NICE 2015/2023): Benefits outweigh risks for most women with symptoms under 60 or within 10 years of menopause (the ''timing hypothesis'' / window of opportunity). Risks: Combined (oestrogen + progestogen): increased breast cancer risk (~1 extra per 1000 women per year — comparable to drinking 1 unit/day or BMI >35). VTE risk increased (especially oral routes — transdermal oestrogen does not increase VTE). Endometrial protection: women with a uterus MUST have progestogen added (oestrogen-only → endometrial hyperplasia/cancer). Mirena IUS provides endometrial protection while allowing systemic oestrogen therapy.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Anatomy & Physiology','Puberty & Menopause','easy','flashcard','What are the hormonal changes of puberty and what drives the pubertal growth spurt?','["Hormonal changes: GnRH pulsatility increases → FSH and LH rise → gonadal steroidogenesis (oestrogen in girls, testosterone in boys). Adrenarche (adrenal DHEA-S) precedes gonadarche. Growth spurt: driven by synergy of sex steroids (oestrogen in girls, testosterone→oestrogen in boys — aromatisation is critical) and growth hormone (GH/IGF-1). Oestrogen is the key hormone for epiphyseal fusion (in both boys and girls — aromatase converts testosterone → oestradiol in male bone). Oestrogen deficiency (aromatase deficiency, AIS in XY) → failure of epiphyseal fusion → very tall stature with continued growth."]','A','The pubertal growth spurt in girls peaks at Tanner stage 3 (mean peak height velocity ~8–9 cm/year); in boys it peaks later at Tanner stage 4 (~10 cm/year). Boys grow for longer and end up ~13 cm taller on average. Oestrogen is the ''epiphyseal fusion hormone'' — this is why: (1) Girls stop growing earlier (oestrogen rise is earlier and steeper). (2) Men with aromatase deficiency or complete AIS (XY) have unfused epiphyses and continue growing into adulthood → very tall. (3) GnRH agonist treatment for central precocious puberty preserves adult height by delaying epiphyseal fusion.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Anatomy & Physiology','Puberty & Menopause','hard','flashcard','Explain menarche — what determines its timing and what is primary amenorrhoea?','["Menarche (first menstrual period): typically occurs at Tanner stage 3–4, ~2 years after thelarche, at a mean age of 12.4 years in the UK. Timing is determined by: genetics (~50% heritable), body composition (critical fat mass — Frisch hypothesis: ~17% body fat required for menarche; not absolute but nutritional status is key), GH/IGF-1 axis, leptin (signals adequate energy stores to hypothalamus → permissive for GnRH pulsatility). Primary amenorrhoea: no period by age 15 (with secondary sexual characteristics) or age 13 (without)."]','A','Primary amenorrhoea evaluation: (1) With secondary sexual characteristics: Müllerian agenesis (Mayer-Rokitansky-Küster-Hauser, MRKH) — normal ovaries, absent uterus/vagina; CAIS (46,XY, female phenotype, absent uterus, testes); cervical stenosis. (2) Without secondary sexual characteristics: Turner syndrome (high FSH/LH); Kallmann syndrome (low FSH/LH, anosmia); constitutional delay; systemic illness (anorexia, hypothyroidism). Investigations: FSH/LH distinguish hypergonadotrophic (Turner) from hypogonadotrophic (Kallmann) causes; pelvic US; karyotype. MRKH: vaginal dilators or surgical vaginoplasty; uterine transplant (experimental) may allow future pregnancy.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Anatomy & Physiology','Puberty & Menopause','medium','flashcard','Describe the perimenopause — definition, hormonal changes, and management.','["Perimenopause (menopausal transition): period of irregular menstrual cycles and vasomotor symptoms beginning before the final menstrual period. Duration: 2–8 years. Hormonal changes: inhibin B falls (earliest change) → FSH rises. Cycles become irregular (variable follicular phase length). Oestrogen may be high, low, or normal — unpredictable. Anovulatory cycles become more common. Fertility declines but pregnancy is still possible → contraception needed until 2 years post-last period (<50 years) or 1 year (≥50 years)."]','A','Symptoms of perimenopause: vasomotor (hot flushes, night sweats in 70–80%), menstrual irregularity, sleep disturbance, mood changes, vaginal dryness, joint pains, brain fog, libido changes. Management: HRT is most effective for vasomotor symptoms. CBT for psychological symptoms and hot flushes (as effective as HRT for some women). Non-hormonal options for vasomotor symptoms: SSRIs/SNRIs (paroxetine, venlafaxine), clonidine (α2 agonist), gabapentin. Novel: Fezolinetant (neurokinin B receptor antagonist) — approved NICE 2023 for vasomotor symptoms when HRT is contraindicated or declined.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Anatomy & Physiology','Puberty & Menopause','hard','flashcard','Describe the effects of long-term oestrogen deficiency on bone and the prevention of postmenopausal osteoporosis.','["Oestrogen deficiency → uncoupling of bone remodelling: osteoclast activity increases (oestrogen normally suppresses RANK-L, inhibits osteoclast differentiation, induces osteoclast apoptosis). Osteoblast activity cannot compensate → net bone loss: ~2–3% per year for 5–7 years post-menopause (''rapid phase''), then 1% per year. Sites: trabecular bone (spine, distal radius) most affected first — compression fractures. Later: cortical bone (hip) → hip fracture (major cause of morbidity/mortality in elderly women)."]','A','Prevention and treatment of postmenopausal osteoporosis: (1) HRT: most effective for prevention if started early post-menopause (reduces fracture risk by 30–50%); effect reversed on stopping. (2) Bisphosphonates (alendronate first-line): inhibit osteoclast activity; reduce vertebral fractures 40–70%, hip fractures 50%; atypical femoral fracture and osteonecrosis of jaw (rare complications — more common with IV bisphosphonates). (3) Denosumab: anti-RANK-L monoclonal antibody — subcutaneous injection every 6 months; powerful; must not be stopped abruptly (rebound bone loss). (4) Teriparatide (PTH analogue): anabolic; for severe osteoporosis. FRAX tool estimates 10-year fracture risk to guide treatment.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Anatomy & Physiology','Puberty & Menopause','medium','flashcard','What is the GnRH pulse generator and how is it regulated throughout the lifespan?','["GnRH pulse generator: network of GnRH neurons (arcuate nucleus) pulsing every 60–120 min, regulated by KNDy neurons (Kisspeptin, Neurokinin B, Dynorphin): NKB accelerates GnRH pulse; dynorphin decelerates pulse; kisspeptin is the final GnRH stimulatory signal. Lifespan changes: Fetal/neonatal (mini-puberty): active; Childhood: suppressed by CNS GABAergic inhibition and opioid tone; Puberty: reactivated; Reproductive years: cyclic, regulated by gonadal steroids. Lactation: suppressed by prolactin/opioids. Menopause: activated maximally (without negative feedback from failed ovaries)."]','A','Mini-puberty of infancy: In the first 3–6 months after birth, the HPG axis is transiently active (gonadotrophin and sex steroid levels are elevated). Important for: testicular descent (males — LH stimulates testosterone, also hCG from placenta prenatally), early penile/clitoral development, programming of reproductive function. Cryptorchidism not spontaneously correcting by 6 months after birth needs urological referral. Mini-puberty ends as CNS GABAergic inhibition kicks in (''juvenile pause''). Understanding this helps in evaluation of infant with ambiguous genitalia (hormone levels during mini-puberty can guide diagnosis).','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed');
